WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:03.890 --> 00:00:06.513
- Welcome everybody to the Board.

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:09.613
I should say, by way, just to start,

00:00:11.350 --> 00:00:13.920
as chair, we've resolved
that at this stage,

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:18.050
the public will be
attending, not in person,

00:00:18.050 --> 00:00:20.450
but actually via the screen.

00:00:20.450 --> 00:00:23.430
But I should add that actually
it's our current intention

00:00:23.430 --> 00:00:26.860
that the next time we meet,
actually, we will be doing that,

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:28.960
enabling the public to join us physically,

00:00:30.895 --> 00:00:32.350
which will be very good.

00:00:32.350 --> 00:00:35.907
We will have a number of
apologies for absence.

00:00:35.907 --> 00:00:39.890
And that includes from Andrew
Morris and Jeremy Townsend,

00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:44.090
Part Carter, Munir
Pirmohammed, Steve Powis

00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:45.993
and Lord Darzi.

00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:52.640
Can I ask if anyone on the Board
has any interest to declare

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:56.150
in addition to those
interests already declared

00:00:56.150 --> 00:00:59.813
or have any interest in any
of the items in this meeting?

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:03.573
Thank you.

00:01:03.573 --> 00:01:05.290
I'll take silence is confirmation

00:01:06.148 --> 00:01:07.600
of no additional interests.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:11.580
And then can I ask the
Board to approve the minutes

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:14.603
of the Board meeting of the 24th of March?

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:18.700
Any objections I can see lots of nodding.

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:19.820
Very good.

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:21.475
Thank you very much.

00:01:21.475 --> 00:01:25.360
What I'd like to do,
if you would humour me,

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:30.360
is I'd just like to provide
a very brief survey overview

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:34.060
of some of the activities
I've been undertaking

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:37.463
as chair over the last five or six weeks.

00:01:39.207 --> 00:01:43.300
And partly actually to share
with you then some reflections

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:46.023
as to areas of focus.

00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:50.810
In terms of meetings, I
think I've met a high volume

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:53.490
of meetings and met now
with all of the seven

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:54.950
regional directors,

00:01:54.950 --> 00:01:57.770
clearly with the NHSE and
NHSI non-exec directors,

00:01:57.770 --> 00:02:00.920
where we've had a number of conversations

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:02.733
with the full executive team,
which has been very good.

00:02:02.733 --> 00:02:07.060
And that's again on multiple occasions.

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:10.510
And then also with other
national directors and HEE

00:02:10.510 --> 00:02:12.563
and NHS Digital colleagues.

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:17.210
In terms of reviews,

00:02:17.210 --> 00:02:19.210
I've had good conversations with Claire.

00:02:19.210 --> 00:02:20.940
So for the fuller stock take,

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:23.930
and you'll see that Claire
and I'm delighted she's here

00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:28.120
is going to give us an
update on her stock take

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:30.220
later on in this meeting.

00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:33.650
A couple of very good
meetings with Gordon Messenger

00:02:33.650 --> 00:02:36.810
concerning his review
conversations with Laura

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:39.610
around her digital technology review.

00:02:39.610 --> 00:02:42.640
And they've all been very informative.

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:47.467
And then on primary care, we've
had one round table so far,

00:02:47.467 --> 00:02:49.910
and there are a couple more round tables

00:02:49.910 --> 00:02:52.930
involving people from outside NHS England,

00:02:52.930 --> 00:02:57.270
actually helping to inform me
about thoughts and thinking

00:02:57.270 --> 00:02:59.610
with regard to the challenges
that we can see currently

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:01.520
in primary care.

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:03.710
And then it won't surprise you
that there've been a number

00:03:03.710 --> 00:03:06.230
of meetings with treasury and number 10.

00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:10.193
And in particular with
the department DHSC,

00:03:11.770 --> 00:03:14.983
I was able to attend
and delighted to attend,

00:03:16.710 --> 00:03:19.160
I think at the inaugural
meeting, actually,

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:23.160
of the meeting of the ICS chairs and COs

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:25.690
so leadership event.

00:03:25.690 --> 00:03:26.990
And that was really, really helpful.

00:03:26.990 --> 00:03:29.712
And I've also met with
several of the ICS leaders

00:03:29.712 --> 00:03:32.220
in different regions
over the last six weeks,

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:34.137
either virtually by way of a Zoom

00:03:34.137 --> 00:03:38.190
or Teams call or actually
on a number of other visits.

00:03:38.190 --> 00:03:41.720
So last weekend I was
up in Blackburn again,

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:44.882
meeting with Lancashire
and South Cumbria ICS,

00:03:44.882 --> 00:03:47.990
and clearly I've also
been to the West Midlands

00:03:47.990 --> 00:03:52.233
and to Birmingham Solihull
and met with the ICS there.

00:03:54.070 --> 00:03:56.720
I'm quite keen on frontline engagement.

00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:00.750
It's something I'd also
be very keen to encourage

00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:02.423
the non-execs to do.

00:04:03.640 --> 00:04:04.910
And so an ambulance shift,

00:04:04.910 --> 00:04:07.280
which I'd mentioned in the past
was very informative to me,

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:11.320
actually, very interesting
reflections following that.

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:13.270
And clearly when I've
gone out into systems

00:04:13.270 --> 00:04:14.470
meeting frontline colleagues,

00:04:14.470 --> 00:04:16.610
either within the hospitals themselves.

00:04:16.610 --> 00:04:19.850
And or actually in the
primary care networks.

00:04:19.850 --> 00:04:22.354
And I did attend actually last week.

00:04:22.354 --> 00:04:24.590
It was a patient's AGM
in a very good practise.

00:04:24.590 --> 00:04:27.310
It was in central London,

00:04:27.310 --> 00:04:31.610
where I did Q and A with about
60 or 70 of the patients.

00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:34.774
Which again was very interesting
to get direct reflection

00:04:34.774 --> 00:04:39.774
from them of their questions
about the health service.

00:04:40.370 --> 00:04:42.330
And then there was some
very important stakeholders

00:04:42.330 --> 00:04:44.820
as well that I've been
keen to meet and have met.

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:49.820
So the NHS assembly, NHS Confederation,

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:52.640
I've met with other arms length bodies.

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:57.640
So NICE with CQC, with AGE
and also with the voluntary

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:01.230
exponent of the voluntary sector.

00:05:01.230 --> 00:05:05.830
So for instance, most recently
with Cancer Research UK,

00:05:05.830 --> 00:05:07.640
and then obviously also the think tanks

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:10.290
and I read quite a lot of their research,

00:05:10.290 --> 00:05:13.418
but I've also met with health
foundation Nuffield Kings Fund

00:05:13.418 --> 00:05:15.283
and so on, which has been very good.

00:05:16.750 --> 00:05:20.623
In terms of themes that I've
increasingly focused on.

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:25.170
Clearly workforce is really
important and we've initiated

00:05:25.170 --> 00:05:28.667
that will come up, I'm
sure in Amanda's CO report.

00:05:28.667 --> 00:05:32.210
But we've initiated work on
long-term planning for workforce

00:05:32.210 --> 00:05:34.220
urgent and emergency care.

00:05:34.220 --> 00:05:39.070
And also primary care access
have become significant themes

00:05:39.070 --> 00:05:40.863
for me to focus upon.

00:05:42.090 --> 00:05:44.560
Also looking at the organisational merge

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:46.480
of the five entities.

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:49.833
And also now beginning to look at estates.

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:57.020
Let me finish with just some
reflections on main areas

00:05:58.150 --> 00:06:00.420
of focus for me.

00:06:00.420 --> 00:06:03.590
And in terms of what
I'm about to highlight

00:06:03.590 --> 00:06:05.630
as main areas of current focus.

00:06:05.630 --> 00:06:08.481
It doesn't mean that
items I don't reference

00:06:08.481 --> 00:06:13.120
are not still truly important.

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:15.920
So for instance, life sciences
and digital and cancer,

00:06:15.920 --> 00:06:18.402
I'm not going to mention
those as my immediate areas of

00:06:18.402 --> 00:06:21.490
focus, but they remain
very important areas

00:06:21.490 --> 00:06:25.520
where clearly NHSE keeps very significant

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:27.690
itself focused upon those.

00:06:27.690 --> 00:06:32.500
So where am my immediate
personal areas of focus?

00:06:32.500 --> 00:06:35.080
I don't think they will
come as a surprise.

00:06:35.080 --> 00:06:39.100
And I shared this with the
Board and members of the Board

00:06:39.100 --> 00:06:41.120
over the past few days.

00:06:41.120 --> 00:06:46.120
Elective backlog, really
important to improve the position

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:49.777
in elective backlog and to
reduce the waiting list.

00:06:49.777 --> 00:06:52.540
And Jim Mackey who's on this call

00:06:52.540 --> 00:06:55.531
is leading on that
initiative with David Sloman

00:06:55.531 --> 00:06:57.580
and we'll get an update on that.

00:06:57.580 --> 00:07:01.670
Urgent and emergency care is the second.

00:07:01.670 --> 00:07:05.160
And then the third is primary care access.

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:07.500
And I add access there
because it strikes me

00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:12.300
that is a very immediate
topic to focus upon

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:14.920
and what we'll have, I'm
sure when Claire speaks

00:07:14.920 --> 00:07:16.500
to us a bit later on in this meeting

00:07:16.500 --> 00:07:17.960
is not just view on access,

00:07:17.960 --> 00:07:21.770
but actually sort of
broader model suggestions

00:07:21.770 --> 00:07:24.810
for how primary care itself might evolve.

00:07:24.810 --> 00:07:27.870
So if those are three
areas that are then two

00:07:27.870 --> 00:07:30.380
very important mechanics,

00:07:30.380 --> 00:07:33.940
which the Board is very focused upon

00:07:33.940 --> 00:07:38.850
and are immediate tasks
that we need to oversee.

00:07:38.850 --> 00:07:40.130
The first I've already mentioned,

00:07:40.130 --> 00:07:42.230
which is the merger of the five entities,

00:07:42.230 --> 00:07:47.230
NHSE, NHSI, Improvement,
NHS Digital, NHSX, and HEE.

00:07:50.090 --> 00:07:52.620
And the second I'll call
it piece of machinery

00:07:52.620 --> 00:07:56.790
or structural machinery,
is going to be giving birth

00:07:56.790 --> 00:07:58.690
essentially to the ICS.

00:07:58.690 --> 00:08:03.140
So that we see the ICSs
come to fruition and operate

00:08:03.140 --> 00:08:05.990
as a fundamental structural
change within the system.

00:08:05.990 --> 00:08:07.960
And clearly the Board will be,

00:08:07.960 --> 00:08:12.463
will necessarily, and importantly
be highly focused on that.

00:08:13.320 --> 00:08:15.550
And then there are two underlying,

00:08:15.550 --> 00:08:18.750
I call them thematic priorities.

00:08:18.750 --> 00:08:20.560
One of which is workforce.

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:22.750
And what I find in my first six weeks

00:08:22.750 --> 00:08:25.470
is the number of questions
where actually you come back

00:08:25.470 --> 00:08:27.460
into the question of workforce.

00:08:27.460 --> 00:08:29.597
And so actually that
remains a very important

00:08:29.597 --> 00:08:32.340
underpinning priority.

00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:36.340
And then the second is a
sort of a one word, thematic.

00:08:36.340 --> 00:08:37.647
But it plays on multiple levels.

00:08:37.647 --> 00:08:41.950
And I think one of the
things that I reflect upon

00:08:41.950 --> 00:08:45.073
after these first six weeks
is the question of capacity.

00:08:45.950 --> 00:08:49.920
And it can either be capacity
of a first order nature,

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:52.540
which can be for instance,
the number of beds,

00:08:52.540 --> 00:08:54.830
or it can be the number
of scanners we have,

00:08:54.830 --> 00:08:57.820
in the system enabling
diagnostic services,

00:08:57.820 --> 00:08:59.900
or it can work on a second order level

00:08:59.900 --> 00:09:04.434
which is actually if one
changes working practises

00:09:04.434 --> 00:09:06.840
or we introduce more technology,

00:09:06.840 --> 00:09:09.510
then actually you can lift
capacity without actually

00:09:09.510 --> 00:09:14.390
necessarily lifting the
physical capacity itself,

00:09:14.390 --> 00:09:17.160
but essentially the operating
capacity can be lifted.

00:09:17.160 --> 00:09:21.031
So I think one of the
areas I'm keen to encourage

00:09:21.031 --> 00:09:24.640
the Board to reflect
upon over future meetings

00:09:24.640 --> 00:09:27.987
will be deep dives into aspects
of capacity for the service

00:09:27.987 --> 00:09:32.987
and how we're positioned and
what we can then do about it.

00:09:33.391 --> 00:09:38.391
Those would be my initial
areas of focus or priorities.

00:09:41.680 --> 00:09:44.510
I shared a number of those
with members of the Board

00:09:45.430 --> 00:09:48.950
earlier today and in separate meetings.

00:09:48.950 --> 00:09:52.800
But obviously I be keen
to take any reflections

00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:57.210
that colleagues have on what they think

00:09:57.210 --> 00:10:00.020
of that prioritisation.

00:10:00.020 --> 00:10:01.710
So let me pause at that point

00:10:01.710 --> 00:10:03.510
for my Chairman's introduction.

00:10:03.510 --> 00:10:06.150
Wait and see if any of
my non-exec colleagues

00:10:06.150 --> 00:10:09.640
have any reflections
or comments and if not,

00:10:09.640 --> 00:10:13.693
then hand to Amanda for her
Chief Executive's update.

00:10:16.610 --> 00:10:18.623
Okay, Amanda, over to you, I think.

00:10:19.580 --> 00:10:20.623
- Great.

00:10:20.623 --> 00:10:22.157
Thank you chair.

00:10:22.157 --> 00:10:24.763
And just, I've got a bit of an echo.

00:10:26.080 --> 00:10:26.990
Should I just try again?

00:10:26.990 --> 00:10:29.101
Oh yeah, that's great, thank you.

00:10:29.101 --> 00:10:30.700
So I'd actually going
to start by just saying,

00:10:30.700 --> 00:10:33.010
of course, on behalf
of the executive group,

00:10:33.010 --> 00:10:36.560
I would like to formally
welcome you to the role Richard,

00:10:36.560 --> 00:10:39.470
although given your extensive engagement,

00:10:39.470 --> 00:10:42.030
both across the NHS
and with the team here,

00:10:42.030 --> 00:10:44.790
I think it is hard to believe
this is only your first Board

00:10:44.790 --> 00:10:46.900
meeting as Chair.

00:10:46.900 --> 00:10:49.360
But I know colleagues
have been hugely grateful

00:10:49.360 --> 00:10:51.720
for the interest you've
already shown in their work

00:10:51.720 --> 00:10:53.920
and the big challenges
the NHS is grappling

00:10:53.920 --> 00:10:56.950
with at the moment and are
very much looking forward

00:10:56.950 --> 00:10:58.840
to working with you.

00:10:58.840 --> 00:10:59.673
If I may mention.

00:10:59.673 --> 00:11:02.420
- Actually, Amanda, can I just
interrupt you for a moment?

00:11:02.420 --> 00:11:05.342
That'll throw you as we're
sitting in different rooms

00:11:05.342 --> 00:11:07.470
and there actually, one thing
I do want to quickly do,

00:11:07.470 --> 00:11:10.783
and it's reciprocity for your, I mean,

00:11:10.783 --> 00:11:13.040
very kind comments to me.

00:11:13.040 --> 00:11:15.933
Just then I'd just like to
thank the Board, actually.

00:11:17.200 --> 00:11:20.040
And I was looking at
the Board's engagement.

00:11:20.040 --> 00:11:24.500
And if I look back at last
year, this Board essentially

00:11:24.500 --> 00:11:29.500
had five formal Board meetings,
two Board strategy meetings.

00:11:31.970 --> 00:11:35.430
Another two full Board,
deep dive sessions.

00:11:35.430 --> 00:11:37.240
And then there'd been separate Board calls

00:11:37.240 --> 00:11:39.940
on a number of sort of
more immediate tasks.

00:11:39.940 --> 00:11:42.340
And then I look at the audit
committee, which has met,

00:11:42.340 --> 00:11:45.200
I think, six times over that period.

00:11:45.200 --> 00:11:47.410
The digital committee has met three times.

00:11:47.410 --> 00:11:49.530
The people remuneration
nominations committee

00:11:49.530 --> 00:11:51.130
has met three times,

00:11:51.130 --> 00:11:53.830
the quality innovation
committee four times.

00:11:53.830 --> 00:11:56.060
And the very good advisory group,

00:11:56.060 --> 00:11:58.270
the joint finance advisory group,

00:11:58.270 --> 00:12:01.080
which supports Julian's finance director

00:12:01.080 --> 00:12:03.730
has met two or three times.

00:12:03.730 --> 00:12:07.030
We have a deep dive next
week on UEC as a Board.

00:12:07.030 --> 00:12:10.460
So again, I'm hugely grateful
to colleagues for that.

00:12:10.460 --> 00:12:12.900
I referenced earlier, Laura's leadership

00:12:12.900 --> 00:12:15.800
in doing the digital technology review.

00:12:15.800 --> 00:12:19.247
We have David Behan and
Jeremy Townsend working

00:12:19.247 --> 00:12:22.580
alongside the execs on the workforce.

00:12:22.580 --> 00:12:25.470
The longer term work workforce planning

00:12:25.470 --> 00:12:30.400
and Wol Kolade is going to
be leading a study or review

00:12:30.400 --> 00:12:31.820
on the question of the state.

00:12:31.820 --> 00:12:34.230
So, you thank me,

00:12:34.230 --> 00:12:36.160
kindly for getting engaged
in the first six weeks.

00:12:36.160 --> 00:12:38.240
But I do want to just thank colleagues

00:12:38.240 --> 00:12:40.230
actually for the amount of work they do.

00:12:40.230 --> 00:12:43.050
That's often not seen, I think,

00:12:43.050 --> 00:12:45.277
in simply the appearance
at the Board meeting.

00:12:45.277 --> 00:12:49.150
I'm very grateful to colleagues
through their commitment.

00:12:51.540 --> 00:12:52.970
Back to you Amanda.

00:12:52.970 --> 00:12:53.896
- Oh, thank you.

00:12:53.896 --> 00:12:55.750
Thank you, Richard, and well said.

00:12:55.750 --> 00:12:59.650
I just wanted to mention
in welcoming you also,

00:12:59.650 --> 00:13:01.490
a couple of other senior appointments

00:13:01.490 --> 00:13:03.860
that we've announced today.

00:13:03.860 --> 00:13:06.570
So we have today announced the appointment

00:13:06.570 --> 00:13:08.393
of a new chief strategy officer,

00:13:08.393 --> 00:13:10.920
and we're going to welcome Chris Hopson,

00:13:10.920 --> 00:13:14.520
who is currently the chief
executive of NHS providers

00:13:14.520 --> 00:13:15.940
on the 13th of June.

00:13:15.940 --> 00:13:18.420
And then the other appointment
that we've announced.

00:13:18.420 --> 00:13:20.770
And I think this is also
a really important one,

00:13:20.770 --> 00:13:22.080
given what you've already said, Richard,

00:13:22.080 --> 00:13:24.830
about your personal focus on primary care

00:13:24.830 --> 00:13:25.990
and primary care access.

00:13:25.990 --> 00:13:29.242
As we have appointed Amanda
Doyle, Dr. Amanda Doyle,

00:13:29.242 --> 00:13:33.240
who is currently the regional
director in the Northwest,

00:13:33.240 --> 00:13:35.640
as the national director for primary care

00:13:35.640 --> 00:13:37.160
and community services.

00:13:37.160 --> 00:13:40.080
So that as a dedicated role
overseeing the leadership

00:13:40.080 --> 00:13:43.495
of that group within NHSE.

00:13:43.495 --> 00:13:48.250
Sadly today, though, is the
day we will say goodbye to Ian,

00:13:48.250 --> 00:13:50.060
at least from the Board.

00:13:50.060 --> 00:13:52.367
He will continue to work here
of course, and work with us,

00:13:52.367 --> 00:13:55.580
supporting us as he has done
brilliantly over the years

00:13:57.678 --> 00:13:59.650
until the end of June.

00:13:59.650 --> 00:14:03.730
But, this is I think,
Ian, your last Board.

00:14:03.730 --> 00:14:06.480
So just to note that then
thank you for all of the things

00:14:06.480 --> 00:14:08.550
you have done over the years

00:14:08.550 --> 00:14:11.160
for which we will miss you enormously.

00:14:11.160 --> 00:14:14.300
But just sort of moving on to the bulk

00:14:14.300 --> 00:14:15.350
of my report, Richard.

00:14:15.350 --> 00:14:16.930
I'd like to start if I may,

00:14:16.930 --> 00:14:21.670
by just providing an update on
the NHS response to COVID 19.

00:14:21.670 --> 00:14:23.560
When we last met,

00:14:23.560 --> 00:14:27.200
we had just begun the spring
booster vaccination campaign,

00:14:27.200 --> 00:14:30.160
and we've now delivered
over 3 million top up doses

00:14:30.160 --> 00:14:32.440
to those in the highest risk groups.

00:14:32.440 --> 00:14:37.150
So that is equivalent to
79% of those eligible age,

00:14:37.150 --> 00:14:38.420
75 and over.

00:14:38.420 --> 00:14:41.410
And given what we know about
the impact of vaccination

00:14:41.410 --> 00:14:43.350
on the severity of disease,

00:14:43.350 --> 00:14:46.660
I think that continues to be alongside

00:14:46.660 --> 00:14:49.360
what we have done with the
COVID medicine delivery units,

00:14:49.360 --> 00:14:52.030
which have now delivered
antiviral and antibody treatment

00:14:52.030 --> 00:14:55.780
to 140,000 of those people
at the highest clinical risk

00:14:55.780 --> 00:14:56.770
who would benefit.

00:14:56.770 --> 00:15:01.730
That combination continues
to be absolutely critical

00:15:01.730 --> 00:15:06.060
to keeping our most vulnerable
people safe from COVID.

00:15:06.060 --> 00:15:09.050
And also is playing into
what we are now seeing

00:15:09.050 --> 00:15:11.770
as a significant reduction
in the number of people

00:15:11.770 --> 00:15:13.690
with COVID in hospitals.

00:15:13.690 --> 00:15:18.690
That peaked as we'll recall at
16,587, on the 6th of April.

00:15:19.430 --> 00:15:23.277
and it's now declined
steadily down to below 5,500.

00:15:24.910 --> 00:15:27.350
So as a result of that sustained fall,

00:15:27.350 --> 00:15:29.350
both in inpatient numbers
and also of course,

00:15:29.350 --> 00:15:31.160
community infections as well.

00:15:31.160 --> 00:15:33.620
And on the advice of the
national incident director

00:15:33.620 --> 00:15:38.620
and his team and obviously
in conjunction with David,

00:15:38.760 --> 00:15:39.790
as Chief Executive,

00:15:39.790 --> 00:15:43.040
I have taken the decision
now to reclassify

00:15:43.040 --> 00:15:44.940
the NHS incident level

00:15:44.940 --> 00:15:47.749
from a level four national
incident to a level three

00:15:47.749 --> 00:15:50.700
regional oversight.

00:15:50.700 --> 00:15:52.180
And that is effective immediately.

00:15:52.180 --> 00:15:54.480
So David and I have
written out this morning

00:15:54.480 --> 00:15:57.150
to local leaders to let
people know about the change.

00:15:57.150 --> 00:16:00.050
And we are speaking to
them later this afternoon

00:16:00.050 --> 00:16:03.880
on a webinar to go through
that in more detail.

00:16:03.880 --> 00:16:05.780
What was addressed in our letter

00:16:05.780 --> 00:16:08.400
is that while we need
obviously to be vigilant

00:16:08.400 --> 00:16:10.300
and be able to step
things back up quickly,

00:16:10.300 --> 00:16:12.050
if it looks like being necessary,

00:16:12.050 --> 00:16:14.490
we are at a point now where as a system,

00:16:14.490 --> 00:16:18.320
we can not step down, but step across.

00:16:18.320 --> 00:16:20.840
So we are putting that resource and time

00:16:20.840 --> 00:16:22.990
that has been focused
on the COVID response

00:16:24.420 --> 00:16:27.740
to the service of our recovery priorities.

00:16:27.740 --> 00:16:30.260
Particularly focusing
on key patient access

00:16:30.260 --> 00:16:31.570
and quality measures.

00:16:31.570 --> 00:16:35.160
And also to reform and
building for the future

00:16:35.160 --> 00:16:38.020
with ICBs and ICSs at the centre.

00:16:38.020 --> 00:16:41.480
We'll say more about
that, I know in a moment.

00:16:41.480 --> 00:16:43.440
So what that means is, of course,

00:16:43.440 --> 00:16:45.540
we are looking at what we can roll back

00:16:45.540 --> 00:16:47.760
in relation to incident
management and reporting

00:16:47.760 --> 00:16:50.260
requirements mindful of what
I was saying about the need

00:16:50.260 --> 00:16:53.420
to turn that back on
quickly, if we need to.

00:16:53.420 --> 00:16:56.300
But for our local colleagues,
it means taking the same.

00:16:56.300 --> 00:16:57.580
And we have seen, as you mentioned it,

00:16:57.580 --> 00:16:58.890
a moment to go in your introduction

00:16:58.890 --> 00:17:01.330
we've seen time and time again,

00:17:01.330 --> 00:17:02.550
the commitment to doing this.

00:17:02.550 --> 00:17:06.540
But that same energetic and
rigorous approach to recovery

00:17:06.540 --> 00:17:08.750
and to delivering the
specific ambition set out

00:17:08.750 --> 00:17:10.410
in the planning guidance for this year

00:17:10.410 --> 00:17:13.330
and the elective recovery
plan published in February

00:17:13.330 --> 00:17:15.810
as they have done to the COVID response.

00:17:15.810 --> 00:17:20.810
So, that same spirit of
innovation and relentless focus

00:17:22.230 --> 00:17:25.580
on doing the right thing for
patients supporting staff,

00:17:25.580 --> 00:17:27.680
but now focused on recovery and reform

00:17:27.680 --> 00:17:31.400
where it has been focused of
course, on the COVID response.

00:17:31.400 --> 00:17:33.640
Although not only on COVID response,

00:17:33.640 --> 00:17:35.380
because one of the things
I wanted to mention

00:17:35.380 --> 00:17:36.540
in my opening remarks,

00:17:36.540 --> 00:17:39.620
is the remarkable things
that I have seen personally,

00:17:39.620 --> 00:17:41.910
as I have been out and
about over the course

00:17:41.910 --> 00:17:45.660
of the last few weeks,
as you have also Richard.

00:17:45.660 --> 00:17:48.650
And the things we have already
seen very much up and running

00:17:48.650 --> 00:17:51.320
across the health service in that spirit

00:17:51.320 --> 00:17:53.780
of recovery and reform.

00:17:53.780 --> 00:17:56.000
But just on the letter,

00:17:56.000 --> 00:18:00.540
we are not adding any new
objectives to the letter.

00:18:00.540 --> 00:18:02.880
We're just asking that
the latest guidance,

00:18:02.880 --> 00:18:05.765
particularly on IPC and
ensuring visiting access

00:18:05.765 --> 00:18:08.410
is implemented in full.

00:18:08.410 --> 00:18:11.110
And I just did want to
say a word on visitors.

00:18:11.110 --> 00:18:14.350
Because allowing visitors
as well as loved ones,

00:18:14.350 --> 00:18:17.160
accompanying those attending
planned appointments

00:18:17.160 --> 00:18:20.330
is really important to patient experience,

00:18:20.330 --> 00:18:22.320
mental health and recovery.

00:18:22.320 --> 00:18:24.740
Not to mention, of course,
the benefit clinicians get

00:18:24.740 --> 00:18:26.836
from having the right
input and into insight

00:18:26.836 --> 00:18:30.080
into their patients, from
those who know them best.

00:18:30.080 --> 00:18:32.200
So as well as the value to patient care,

00:18:32.200 --> 00:18:34.330
this is about the core NHS values

00:18:34.330 --> 00:18:37.251
of compassion, dignity, respect.

00:18:37.251 --> 00:18:39.912
And whilst COVID is still
circulating the community,

00:18:39.912 --> 00:18:43.310
of course, we will understand
the need to balance risk.

00:18:43.310 --> 00:18:47.580
Our starting point has to be
what do patients want to need,

00:18:47.580 --> 00:18:50.410
and a vast majority of
inpatients will want

00:18:50.410 --> 00:18:53.269
and will need to see visitors.

00:18:53.269 --> 00:18:57.870
So, colleagues are, of
course, I think well aware,

00:18:57.870 --> 00:19:00.310
and we'll talk more about
it in the Board meeting

00:19:00.310 --> 00:19:04.370
today of our recovery challenge.

00:19:04.370 --> 00:19:06.840
But I did want to pull
out just a few things

00:19:06.840 --> 00:19:08.270
by way of introduction.

00:19:08.270 --> 00:19:10.890
So, firstly on cancer services.

00:19:10.890 --> 00:19:14.680
They've obviously been prioritised
throughout the pandemic

00:19:14.680 --> 00:19:15.890
for obvious reasons.

00:19:15.890 --> 00:19:18.710
But what we do know is that
many people were reluctant

00:19:18.710 --> 00:19:19.980
to come forward.

00:19:19.980 --> 00:19:22.190
And a lot of people said, in fact,

00:19:22.190 --> 00:19:24.960
about half of people said
they didn't come forward

00:19:24.960 --> 00:19:26.600
even if they had worrying symptoms

00:19:26.600 --> 00:19:29.030
because they didn't
want to burden the NHS.

00:19:29.030 --> 00:19:31.473
Now we've said throughout the pandemic,

00:19:31.473 --> 00:19:32.837
people should come forward

00:19:32.837 --> 00:19:33.780
if they were worried about their health.

00:19:33.780 --> 00:19:35.950
But the good news is that now that trend

00:19:35.950 --> 00:19:38.000
has very much reversed.

00:19:38.000 --> 00:19:40.440
So we now have more
people than ever before

00:19:40.440 --> 00:19:42.300
coming forward for cancer checks

00:19:42.300 --> 00:19:47.090
with a quarter of a million
urgent referrals in March alone.

00:19:47.090 --> 00:19:50.120
And that is the most on
record for a single month.

00:19:50.120 --> 00:19:52.330
And we really want that to continue.

00:19:52.330 --> 00:19:55.670
And that is why our
campaigns and awareness work,

00:19:55.670 --> 00:19:59.200
which we continue to do
with our fantastic partners

00:19:59.200 --> 00:20:01.530
and charities is so important.

00:20:01.530 --> 00:20:03.860
And I particularly wanted
to highlight the news.

00:20:03.860 --> 00:20:07.600
You might have seen this
morning that a record 25,000 men

00:20:07.600 --> 00:20:09.870
were sent for prostate checks in March.

00:20:09.870 --> 00:20:11.800
And that's because our campaign,

00:20:11.800 --> 00:20:13.670
which we launched in
February in partnership

00:20:13.670 --> 00:20:17.470
with Prostate Cancer UK, and
a number of famous faces,

00:20:17.470 --> 00:20:19.930
has encouraged half a million men

00:20:19.930 --> 00:20:21.700
to check their risk online.

00:20:21.700 --> 00:20:23.930
So as we've seen time and time, again,

00:20:23.930 --> 00:20:25.500
better awareness of symptoms

00:20:25.500 --> 00:20:29.660
and encouragement to come
forward, saves lives.

00:20:29.660 --> 00:20:31.440
I'm sure the Board will also just want

00:20:31.440 --> 00:20:35.560
to join me then and
commending the incredible work

00:20:35.560 --> 00:20:38.320
that Deborah James has
done, to raise awareness

00:20:38.320 --> 00:20:41.650
of bowel cancer over the last six years.

00:20:41.650 --> 00:20:44.320
And this is clearly a very difficult time

00:20:44.320 --> 00:20:46.830
for Deborah and for her family.

00:20:46.830 --> 00:20:50.270
But her willingness to share
her experiences since diagnosis

00:20:50.270 --> 00:20:55.140
and even now, has undoubtedly saved lives

00:20:55.140 --> 00:20:57.010
and it will continue to do so.

00:20:57.010 --> 00:20:58.863
So, this week alone,

00:20:58.863 --> 00:21:03.863
we've seen an estimated
82,000 extra visits

00:21:04.090 --> 00:21:07.770
to the NHS bowel cancer
page on our website.

00:21:07.770 --> 00:21:11.430
And for most people who
come forward for a check,

00:21:11.430 --> 00:21:15.335
it's not cancer, but for
those who do get a diagnosis,

00:21:15.335 --> 00:21:19.770
getting treatment as
early as possible is key.

00:21:19.770 --> 00:21:23.980
So, anyone watching this,
please, if you are worried,

00:21:23.980 --> 00:21:28.520
do come forward because the
sooner you do the better,

00:21:28.520 --> 00:21:30.693
it could save your life.

00:21:31.600 --> 00:21:35.730
So, just secondly, on urgent
and emergency care services,

00:21:35.730 --> 00:21:39.910
we know they are still
under immense pressure.

00:21:39.910 --> 00:21:41.370
What that means is the individuals

00:21:41.370 --> 00:21:43.570
who deliver them are under immense

00:21:43.570 --> 00:21:45.469
and unsustainable pressure as well.

00:21:45.469 --> 00:21:48.890
And I'm thinking here particularly
about ambulance staff,

00:21:48.890 --> 00:21:49.840
as well as of course,

00:21:49.840 --> 00:21:53.140
those in emergency
departments and acute wards.

00:21:53.140 --> 00:21:54.230
Demand is high.

00:21:54.230 --> 00:21:58.530
Last month was the busiest ever
April for ambulance services

00:21:58.530 --> 00:22:01.080
in terms of calls and
category one incidents

00:22:01.080 --> 00:22:02.823
and the second busiest for A&amp;Es.

00:22:03.720 --> 00:22:05.040
But that isn't the whole story.

00:22:05.040 --> 00:22:08.550
And we've talked about
this as a Board before.

00:22:08.550 --> 00:22:11.410
We did see some improvements
from March to April

00:22:11.410 --> 00:22:13.470
on key measures like
the A&amp;E treatment time,

00:22:13.470 --> 00:22:16.280
handover delays, and
ambulance response times,

00:22:16.280 --> 00:22:18.070
across all categories.

00:22:18.070 --> 00:22:21.670
But the further rise in 12 hour admissions

00:22:21.670 --> 00:22:23.680
from A&amp;E to ward underlines,

00:22:23.680 --> 00:22:27.660
that flow is still a real issue here.

00:22:27.660 --> 00:22:30.550
And we've talked before about
how we can trace the line

00:22:30.550 --> 00:22:33.484
from delayed discharges
all the way through

00:22:33.484 --> 00:22:37.300
to slower ambulance response times.

00:22:37.300 --> 00:22:39.500
And Matthew Taylor who's
the chief executive

00:22:40.595 --> 00:22:43.380
of the NHS Confederation was
on the radio last week saying

00:22:43.380 --> 00:22:46.780
that increasing capacity in social care

00:22:46.780 --> 00:22:49.700
to support discharge was
where he would concentrate.

00:22:49.700 --> 00:22:51.350
And I have to say that certainly matches

00:22:51.350 --> 00:22:53.930
what local leaders are telling us.

00:22:53.930 --> 00:22:55.763
- And Amanda's, sorry.

00:22:57.070 --> 00:22:58.260
And we have I reference.

00:22:58.260 --> 00:23:02.076
We have this deep dive next
I think it is next Wednesday

00:23:02.076 --> 00:23:03.950
on urgent emergency care,

00:23:03.950 --> 00:23:05.410
which I'm really looking forward to.

00:23:05.410 --> 00:23:10.410
And I think our pressure
point are deep inquiry.

00:23:10.900 --> 00:23:15.212
What are the actions, or
what are the quick wins?

00:23:15.212 --> 00:23:17.204
And then what are the not
necessarily the quick wins?

00:23:17.204 --> 00:23:19.510
But that sort of I'll call
them, you use the phrase,

00:23:19.510 --> 00:23:22.030
me the signature moves as well

00:23:22.030 --> 00:23:25.010
that might make for more
medium term improvement.

00:23:25.010 --> 00:23:27.210
But it's as much, what can we do?

00:23:27.210 --> 00:23:29.750
What can we look at doing
now that can quite quickly

00:23:29.750 --> 00:23:31.800
try and get on top of this issue?

00:23:31.800 --> 00:23:34.710
And there are some very
difficult systemic problems

00:23:34.710 --> 00:23:37.191
that sit there, but are there
some sort of interventions

00:23:37.191 --> 00:23:40.300
that we can go after that
might help more quickly

00:23:40.300 --> 00:23:41.730
to improve the picture.

00:23:41.730 --> 00:23:43.810
So I'm looking forward to the
deep dive as colleagues are,

00:23:43.810 --> 00:23:45.250
I think next Wednesday.

00:23:45.250 --> 00:23:46.950
- Yeah, so Richard you're right.

00:23:46.950 --> 00:23:49.960
I mean, clearly what I
was going to go on to say

00:23:49.960 --> 00:23:51.810
is there are some big questions

00:23:51.810 --> 00:23:54.250
that we need to look at
around physical capacity.

00:23:54.250 --> 00:23:56.660
Whether we've got the right
number of hospital beds,

00:23:56.660 --> 00:24:00.670
treatment spaces in A&amp;E and
in other urgent care settings.

00:24:00.670 --> 00:24:02.100
We're obviously actively looking at that.

00:24:02.100 --> 00:24:04.450
Now, the second big
question we need to look at,

00:24:04.450 --> 00:24:06.110
is have we got the workforce?

00:24:06.110 --> 00:24:07.470
And when I say we,

00:24:07.470 --> 00:24:09.980
I do mean actually, very much,

00:24:09.980 --> 00:24:13.460
we as in the health and care team.

00:24:13.460 --> 00:24:16.170
Because as I say, an
awful lot of the challenge

00:24:16.170 --> 00:24:19.410
is in the domiciliary care support.

00:24:19.410 --> 00:24:23.290
And that is mostly provided
by colleagues in social care.

00:24:23.290 --> 00:24:25.580
So, there are some really big questions

00:24:25.580 --> 00:24:27.945
that we obviously need
to work through that,

00:24:27.945 --> 00:24:29.410
go talk to the medium and long term.

00:24:29.410 --> 00:24:30.940
But in the meantime,

00:24:30.940 --> 00:24:34.280
this is going to require
absolutely continued collaborative

00:24:34.280 --> 00:24:37.220
work at system level to do
everything we can to ensure

00:24:37.220 --> 00:24:39.560
people are treated in
the right place for them,

00:24:39.560 --> 00:24:41.070
and at the right time.

00:24:41.070 --> 00:24:44.340
And do, as you say, everything we can

00:24:44.340 --> 00:24:46.930
to make sure we're
collectively relieving pressure

00:24:46.930 --> 00:24:48.700
on the urgent emergency care pathways

00:24:48.700 --> 00:24:50.832
and whether that's the
continued utilisation

00:24:50.832 --> 00:24:53.896
of the new urgent
community response teams,

00:24:53.896 --> 00:24:57.000
the two hour response
teams that we've set up

00:24:57.000 --> 00:24:58.410
across the country.

00:24:58.410 --> 00:25:00.750
Whether that's helping as
many people as possible

00:25:00.750 --> 00:25:02.430
through urgent treatment centres,

00:25:02.430 --> 00:25:04.590
direct bookings into community pharmacy,

00:25:04.590 --> 00:25:05.930
or other community settings,

00:25:05.930 --> 00:25:08.217
where they're likely to
be seen more quickly.

00:25:08.217 --> 00:25:11.420
Making sure we've done
things like fully reinstate,

00:25:11.420 --> 00:25:14.490
same day emergency care,
acute frailty services.

00:25:14.490 --> 00:25:17.880
So people can get help
directly without necessarily

00:25:17.880 --> 00:25:21.021
having to go through
A&amp;E but tailored support

00:25:21.021 --> 00:25:23.050
for what they need.

00:25:23.050 --> 00:25:24.053
But also making sure of course,

00:25:24.053 --> 00:25:26.240
that we leverage the
significant investment

00:25:26.240 --> 00:25:28.260
that we are making this
year in virtual towards

00:25:28.260 --> 00:25:30.720
as part of that wider
effort to speed up discharge

00:25:30.720 --> 00:25:33.330
and provide alternatives to admission

00:25:33.330 --> 00:25:36.040
as well as relief
pressure on bed capacity.

00:25:36.040 --> 00:25:39.220
So, Richard, I think lots to
talk about in the deep dive.

00:25:39.220 --> 00:25:44.220
But I thought it was just
important to put on, well,

00:25:44.223 --> 00:25:45.103
I suppose on record.

00:25:45.103 --> 00:25:46.265
- Yeah.

00:25:46.265 --> 00:25:47.098
- The recognition we have the impact

00:25:47.098 --> 00:25:48.709
that is having on our workforce now,

00:25:48.709 --> 00:25:50.464
as well as on our patients course.

00:25:50.464 --> 00:25:51.533
- Thanks, Amanda, yeah.

00:25:51.533 --> 00:25:54.280
Just thirdly, then on planned care.

00:25:54.280 --> 00:25:56.870
Monthly performance data for
March published last week

00:25:56.870 --> 00:25:58.870
showed we continue to
make progress towards

00:25:58.870 --> 00:26:01.210
the first milestone in the
elective recovery plan.

00:26:01.210 --> 00:26:04.390
That's the elimination of over
two year waits other than,

00:26:04.390 --> 00:26:06.480
of course by patient choice
or at height for highly

00:26:06.480 --> 00:26:08.860
specialised treatment by July.

00:26:08.860 --> 00:26:11.310
We're going to talk about
that later in Jim is with us.

00:26:11.310 --> 00:26:14.100
He's been leading that work
with Pauline and others.

00:26:14.100 --> 00:26:18.180
So we saw a record March
against key diagnostic test

00:26:18.180 --> 00:26:22.980
activity up by 217,000
on the previous month.

00:26:22.980 --> 00:26:24.600
And although we know, as I've just said,

00:26:24.600 --> 00:26:26.550
that April was a very challenging month

00:26:26.550 --> 00:26:29.400
due to urgent care demand and COVID peak.

00:26:29.400 --> 00:26:32.730
The indications are that
we did remain on track

00:26:32.730 --> 00:26:35.470
and that's thanks to an
immense amount of hard work

00:26:35.470 --> 00:26:39.360
and continued innovation
by teams across the NHS.

00:26:39.360 --> 00:26:40.193
I just said,

00:26:40.193 --> 00:26:42.670
I've been lucky enough to
see a lot of that myself

00:26:42.670 --> 00:26:45.410
since we last met, coincidentally

00:26:45.410 --> 00:26:46.740
and on a different day,

00:26:46.740 --> 00:26:49.450
I was up in Blackburn
a couple of weeks ago.

00:26:49.450 --> 00:26:50.760
