since then is inflation
running at much higher levels

00:39:11.770 --> 00:39:13.210
than the level anticipated

00:39:13.210 --> 00:39:16.100
in the spending round last November.

00:39:16.100 --> 00:39:18.560
But we've said to the NHS,

00:39:18.560 --> 00:39:20.770
where there are excess inflation costs,

00:39:20.770 --> 00:39:23.723
we'll need to deal with those nationally,

00:39:24.750 --> 00:39:27.300
but we want balanced
plans putting that aside.

00:39:27.300 --> 00:39:29.160
Now we've done a lot
of work with providers

00:39:29.160 --> 00:39:31.200
and commissioners to
identify what those inflation

00:39:31.200 --> 00:39:34.004
pressures are as they appear energy bills,

00:39:34.004 --> 00:39:39.004
PFI contracts that are tied
to the retail price index,

00:39:39.960 --> 00:39:41.080
or certain other contracts

00:39:41.080 --> 00:39:43.599
that are tied to the retail price index.

00:39:43.599 --> 00:39:47.820
The fact that food prices
are actually higher.

00:39:47.820 --> 00:39:52.290
The fact that we've seen
eight, nine, 10% growth

00:39:52.290 --> 00:39:56.250
in the price for continuing
healthcare packages

00:39:56.250 --> 00:40:01.250
that is directly driven
by the price uplifts

00:40:02.150 --> 00:40:05.240
that are going on in social
care as government policy

00:40:05.240 --> 00:40:08.491
also tries to align publicly
funded and privately

00:40:08.491 --> 00:40:12.720
funded rates, at least
bring them closer into line.

00:40:12.720 --> 00:40:16.010
Fuel prices are higher
for the ambulance service.

00:40:16.010 --> 00:40:21.010
So, we are going to allocate
another one and a half billion

00:40:21.050 --> 00:40:24.793
pounds to systems to cover those costs.

00:40:25.670 --> 00:40:30.670
We are therefore now in the
process of finalising plans

00:40:32.220 --> 00:40:35.170
with systems and making good progress

00:40:35.170 --> 00:40:38.380
in finalising those plans.

00:40:38.380 --> 00:40:39.373
I mean,

00:40:39.373 --> 00:40:40.960
clearly the other thing
that has changed compared

00:40:40.960 --> 00:40:43.620
to some of the assumptions in September

00:40:43.620 --> 00:40:48.620
is the level of COVID as
we went through January,

00:40:50.020 --> 00:40:53.960
February, March, April,
May, as Amanda alluded to.

00:40:53.960 --> 00:40:58.420
That clearly is having an
impact on the global level

00:40:58.420 --> 00:41:00.400
of activity currently being delivered

00:41:01.520 --> 00:41:04.830
compared with the assumptions
when we originally reached

00:41:04.830 --> 00:41:06.510
the agreement with
government back in September.

00:41:06.510 --> 00:41:09.770
So, we are just looking at what that means

00:41:09.770 --> 00:41:14.133
and how that plays through
as we seek to finalise plans,

00:41:15.450 --> 00:41:18.970
which we are aiming to do
before the end of June,

00:41:18.970 --> 00:41:21.670
so that we can finish the first quarter,

00:41:21.670 --> 00:41:24.672
get the establishment of
integrated care Boards

00:41:24.672 --> 00:41:29.672
sort of properly on a
good firm foundation.

00:41:34.440 --> 00:41:35.273
- Thank you.

00:41:35.273 --> 00:41:37.730
And as you look forward, Julian,

00:41:37.730 --> 00:41:42.040
and working with the
finance advisory group

00:41:42.040 --> 00:41:45.900
and also working through with
the audit and risk committee,

00:41:45.900 --> 00:41:47.600
one of the things I know
you're going to be thinking

00:41:47.600 --> 00:41:49.950
and looking at is that
oversight of the engagement

00:41:49.950 --> 00:41:53.110
with the ICS's as the funds now move,

00:41:53.110 --> 00:41:55.490
essentially, through those
as if I call them pivots

00:41:55.490 --> 00:41:56.560
in the structure.

00:41:56.560 --> 00:41:59.450
So that's presuming a
significant feature of the work

00:41:59.450 --> 00:42:01.820
of the audit risk committee
and your joint finance

00:42:01.820 --> 00:42:05.480
advisory group as we go through
the next 12 months or so.

00:42:05.480 --> 00:42:06.680
- Yeah, I mean,

00:42:06.680 --> 00:42:08.360
the key thing is how do we keep evolving

00:42:08.360 --> 00:42:09.850
the financial framework,

00:42:09.850 --> 00:42:12.880
consistent with the goals
that we want to deliver

00:42:12.880 --> 00:42:17.323
and make sure financial
flows, the payment mechanisms,

00:42:18.840 --> 00:42:23.110
incentivize support the goals
and priorities that we have.

00:42:23.110 --> 00:42:24.760
- Yeah, yeah.

00:42:24.760 --> 00:42:29.063
Any comments or questions for Julian?

00:42:30.520 --> 00:42:33.260
It's good to see that the
we've been having real go,

00:42:33.260 --> 00:42:35.786
trying to assess and deal with,

00:42:35.786 --> 00:42:38.290
and then actually to fund
that inflation pressure,

00:42:38.290 --> 00:42:40.091
as far as we can see it,

00:42:40.091 --> 00:42:40.924
that's coming through the various systems.

00:42:40.924 --> 00:42:41.880
That's been very good.

00:42:42.895 --> 00:42:44.580
Oh, Wol, your hand's up, good.

00:42:44.580 --> 00:42:46.664
Sorry, there are so many screens.

00:42:46.664 --> 00:42:48.650
I sometimes don't spot the
yellow hand as people wave at me,

00:42:48.650 --> 00:42:50.350
but Wol, hi.

00:42:50.350 --> 00:42:51.990
- I shall shout next time.

00:42:51.990 --> 00:42:53.465
Again, very briefly,

00:42:53.465 --> 00:42:56.460
there's a very tiny comment on CapEx

00:42:58.963 --> 00:43:01.117
and we seem to have got
a small under spend.

00:43:01.117 --> 00:43:03.810
But is it worth just saying
a little bit more about CapEx

00:43:03.810 --> 00:43:07.700
going forward and how we're
feeling about some of the real

00:43:07.700 --> 00:43:10.483
pressures around the state of our estates?

00:43:11.410 --> 00:43:12.243
I'm thinking about this,

00:43:12.243 --> 00:43:14.560
given that Richard, just
happened to be a task?

00:43:16.610 --> 00:43:17.550
- Sure.

00:43:17.550 --> 00:43:22.460
I mean, broadly, as we look ahead,

00:43:22.460 --> 00:43:24.810
we will be giving about 4 billion pounds

00:43:24.810 --> 00:43:27.680
to systems as sort of core allocations

00:43:28.680 --> 00:43:32.510
to work out how they
can, it's their choice

00:43:32.510 --> 00:43:36.440
as to how they invest,
how they spend that money.

00:43:36.440 --> 00:43:39.883
And most of it will go on
maintaining the estate,

00:43:41.310 --> 00:43:45.440
replacing equipment. Minor,

00:43:45.440 --> 00:43:47.583
I mean, in the grand scheme of things,

00:43:49.310 --> 00:43:52.570
sort of modernization programmes.

00:43:52.570 --> 00:43:57.570
I mean, clearly there are
priorities for us to stabilise

00:43:57.900 --> 00:44:01.870
what was over the back
half of the last decade

00:44:01.870 --> 00:44:06.119
rapidly growing level of
kind of backlog maintenance.

00:44:06.119 --> 00:44:09.400
And sort of critical infrastructure risk.

00:44:09.400 --> 00:44:14.210
And, I think with the
capital we've got, I mean,

00:44:14.210 --> 00:44:15.150
in all honesty,

00:44:15.150 --> 00:44:17.080
the aim would have to be
to kind of stabilise that.

00:44:17.080 --> 00:44:19.672
I don't think it is going
to make massive inroads

00:44:19.672 --> 00:44:22.070
into that at this stage.

00:44:22.070 --> 00:44:23.880
I mean, we've clearly got
a challenge we need to deal

00:44:23.880 --> 00:44:27.880
with in particular with those trusts,

00:44:27.880 --> 00:44:32.880
where we have very large
element of rapidly aerated

00:44:35.920 --> 00:44:37.233
concrete planks.

00:44:38.600 --> 00:44:40.350
Where we are investing more money

00:44:40.350 --> 00:44:42.960
to subject we'll have to come back to,

00:44:42.960 --> 00:44:45.789
as we look at the sort of
second half of this decade.

00:44:45.789 --> 00:44:48.880
Clearly, there's quite a lot of investment

00:44:48.880 --> 00:44:53.020
we are now putting into
diagnostic capacity.

00:44:53.020 --> 00:44:56.420
Which will improve the
position on, I mean,

00:44:56.420 --> 00:45:01.420
literally just our
capacity for scans imaging

00:45:03.052 --> 00:45:08.052
and terms of digitising
pathology and imaging networks,

00:45:08.730 --> 00:45:12.143
which will significantly help
our productivity as well.

00:45:13.900 --> 00:45:17.600
We're putting specific money
into elective capacity.

00:45:17.600 --> 00:45:20.767
Again, the government has
backed us with about one

00:45:20.767 --> 00:45:22.750
and a half billion pounds over the course

00:45:22.750 --> 00:45:24.233
of the next three years,

00:45:25.690 --> 00:45:28.990
a lot of which we need to
use to create more protective

00:45:28.990 --> 00:45:32.990
elective capacity, not just
deal to deal with the backlogs,

00:45:32.990 --> 00:45:35.310
but actually to make us more
resilient against future shocks

00:45:35.310 --> 00:45:38.490
and indeed often just
normal winter pressures.

00:45:38.490 --> 00:45:40.210
And it's clearly a big investment

00:45:40.210 --> 00:45:41.890
that we're putting into technology

00:45:41.890 --> 00:45:44.380
with the aim of basically by 2025,

00:45:44.380 --> 00:45:48.810
having no provider without
an electronic patient record

00:45:49.940 --> 00:45:51.410
alongside other things.

00:45:51.410 --> 00:45:54.690
But, we will have ongoing
challenges just about the quality

00:45:54.690 --> 00:45:59.373
of the estate, which we are
going to have to come back to.

00:46:00.410 --> 00:46:04.770
- Wol, I'm very grateful
for you to you for leading

00:46:05.819 --> 00:46:08.950
on that piece of work,
working alongside Julian.

00:46:08.950 --> 00:46:11.810
And I think it goes back
to that theme of capacity

00:46:11.810 --> 00:46:13.730
that I want us to focus.

00:46:13.730 --> 00:46:16.530
I know we all want to focus on actually,

00:46:16.530 --> 00:46:18.625
and there's that physical capacity point.

00:46:18.625 --> 00:46:22.440
Which extends across whether
it's the new hospital build

00:46:22.440 --> 00:46:25.100
programme or the quality
of the current state,

00:46:25.100 --> 00:46:28.580
where's the increase in
the number of our community

00:46:28.580 --> 00:46:30.250
diagnostic centres that have been funded,

00:46:30.250 --> 00:46:32.240
have been rolled out, which clearly helps.

00:46:32.240 --> 00:46:33.827
But there's a much broader
and bigger question, I think,

00:46:33.827 --> 00:46:36.416
that we need to get our arms around.

00:46:36.416 --> 00:46:40.503
And I'm very grateful to you
for be willing to lead on that.

00:46:41.781 --> 00:46:43.560
There's one, actually,

00:46:43.560 --> 00:46:46.170
it's sort of going to lurch
into this conversation

00:46:46.170 --> 00:46:48.720
as a tangent, which I apologise.

00:46:48.720 --> 00:46:51.250
But I do keep reflecting Amanda,

00:46:51.250 --> 00:46:53.320
it's on the statistic
that I think you shared

00:46:53.320 --> 00:46:54.870
with me in the past, certainly,

00:46:56.281 --> 00:46:57.770
probably with a number
of the other colleagues.

00:46:57.770 --> 00:47:02.770
That it's the number of
patients who caught COVID

00:47:03.740 --> 00:47:05.950
and were hospitalised.

00:47:05.950 --> 00:47:08.799
And it's quite a
remarkable number, I think,

00:47:08.799 --> 00:47:12.470
because I think it's over 700,000 post 730

00:47:12.470 --> 00:47:15.800
or something like that thousand patients

00:47:15.800 --> 00:47:20.800
came through our hospital beds
through that two year period.

00:47:22.830 --> 00:47:24.240
And it's remarkable really when one thinks

00:47:24.240 --> 00:47:26.410
that the capacity, the bed capacity

00:47:26.410 --> 00:47:28.310
is about a hundred thousand.

00:47:28.310 --> 00:47:32.110
So you've actually sort of used
seven times the total volume

00:47:32.110 --> 00:47:35.019
of our beds in that two year period

00:47:35.019 --> 00:47:39.050
to manage the consequence of the pandemic

00:47:39.050 --> 00:47:40.260
for those patients who caught it,

00:47:40.260 --> 00:47:41.970
who actually required hospitalisation.

00:47:41.970 --> 00:47:44.220
But it's a very significant, I think,

00:47:44.220 --> 00:47:47.368
example of the degree of
incremental, more than incremental,

00:47:47.368 --> 00:47:50.310
additional pressure
that the health services

00:47:50.310 --> 00:47:51.310
had to deal with it.

00:47:57.610 --> 00:47:58.443
Okay.

00:47:58.443 --> 00:48:01.998
David, from an operation
journey from a financial update,

00:48:01.998 --> 00:48:06.071
is there anything else you
would want to add at this stage?

00:48:06.071 --> 00:48:07.250
- No.

00:48:07.250 --> 00:48:09.170
I mean, look clearly
for us, the priority is,

00:48:09.170 --> 00:48:12.850
nailing credible realistic plans,

00:48:12.850 --> 00:48:14.740
and then just focusing on delivery

00:48:14.740 --> 00:48:16.610
and then turning our attention
to how we set the right

00:48:16.610 --> 00:48:19.390
framework for 23, 24 and beyond.

00:48:19.390 --> 00:48:20.593
- Yeah, I totally agree.

00:48:25.230 --> 00:48:26.580
You're on mute Richard.

00:48:29.650 --> 00:48:31.950
- Well, after all this time
I should have managed that,

00:48:31.950 --> 00:48:34.110
shouldn't I really, but
anyway, apologies guys.

00:48:34.110 --> 00:48:37.660
I was going to say, Julian,
thank you very good.

00:48:37.660 --> 00:48:39.920
Well, said at the end there,

00:48:39.920 --> 00:48:41.383
I'm going to move us on
if that's all right onto,

00:48:42.460 --> 00:48:45.195
I think it's item five on the agenda.

00:48:45.195 --> 00:48:48.010
So it's maternity and
neonatal services update.

00:48:48.010 --> 00:48:49.640
Ruth, welcome.

00:48:49.640 --> 00:48:51.107
And I think you've got a
number of your team with you.

00:48:51.107 --> 00:48:54.337
You might just want to
introduce them to us.

00:48:54.337 --> 00:48:56.450
But I think you're going to
provide us with an overview

00:48:56.450 --> 00:48:59.010
to the NHS response to Ockenden

00:49:01.550 --> 00:49:05.330
and also perhaps some
refresh of the strategy

00:49:05.330 --> 00:49:07.360
towards maternity and the neonatal care.

00:49:07.360 --> 00:49:09.167
But Ruth, over to you,

00:49:09.167 --> 00:49:10.541
if that's right to introduce your team

00:49:10.541 --> 00:49:12.370
and then to set the conversation up.

00:49:12.370 --> 00:49:14.060
- Thanks very much chair.

00:49:14.060 --> 00:49:16.530
With me, I've got Aidan Fowler,

00:49:16.530 --> 00:49:19.200
I'm looking at this screen.

00:49:19.200 --> 00:49:22.360
I've got Jacqueline
Dunkley-Bent, Phoebe, Matthew,

00:49:22.360 --> 00:49:27.360
Sarah-Jane, Duncan, all
here to provide bits

00:49:27.670 --> 00:49:30.240
of the update for you.

00:49:30.240 --> 00:49:34.183
So since the last Board we
have now the final report

00:49:35.730 --> 00:49:36.967
from Donna Ockenden on maternity services

00:49:36.967 --> 00:49:40.720
at Shrewsbury and Telford Hospital.

00:49:40.720 --> 00:49:45.720
It sets out terrible failings
suffered by many families.

00:49:46.870 --> 00:49:49.820
And I am deeply sorry for the loss,

00:49:49.820 --> 00:49:53.460
the heartbreak that each of
these women and their families

00:49:53.460 --> 00:49:54.917
have had to endure.

00:49:55.940 --> 00:50:00.770
I pay particular tribute
to Riannan and Richard,

00:50:00.770 --> 00:50:02.843
Kaley and Colin.

00:50:03.900 --> 00:50:07.360
Two families torn apart with the deaths

00:50:07.360 --> 00:50:09.483
of Kate and Pippa.

00:50:11.120 --> 00:50:16.120
They worked very hard
tirelessly to ensure the NHS

00:50:18.280 --> 00:50:19.623
has this report.

00:50:20.530 --> 00:50:25.530
Thank you to Donna Ockenden
and her team of reviewers

00:50:25.550 --> 00:50:29.700
for producing this Ockenden final report.

00:50:29.700 --> 00:50:33.070
I know that the NHS in
England is committed

00:50:33.070 --> 00:50:36.010
to ensuring that the
unacceptable experiences

00:50:36.010 --> 00:50:38.583
of these families are responded to.

00:50:40.160 --> 00:50:42.650
The report provides a call to action

00:50:42.650 --> 00:50:45.590
for all those who are part of the system.

00:50:45.590 --> 00:50:48.050
And we owe it to the families involved

00:50:48.050 --> 00:50:52.323
to learn and improve
maternity care across England.

00:50:53.620 --> 00:50:56.727
I am clear about what we need to do,

00:50:56.727 --> 00:50:59.920
and my colleagues will set
out three themes of actions

00:50:59.920 --> 00:51:02.730
to drive the necessary improvements.

00:51:02.730 --> 00:51:04.710
Oversight insurance,

00:51:04.710 --> 00:51:06.770
leadership and culture,

00:51:06.770 --> 00:51:09.330
investment in workforce.

00:51:09.330 --> 00:51:11.800
But first I'd like to turn to Aidan Fowler

00:51:11.800 --> 00:51:14.690
to update us on the Ockenden report.

00:51:14.690 --> 00:51:15.613
Thank you, Aidan.

00:51:16.460 --> 00:51:18.071
- Thank you, Ruth.

00:51:18.071 --> 00:51:19.060
And thank you chair.

00:51:19.060 --> 00:51:24.060
So I just wanted to give what
is a relatively brief summary

00:51:24.430 --> 00:51:28.470
of the very extensive
Ockenden review of maternity

00:51:28.470 --> 00:51:29.500
in Shrewsbury and Telford,

00:51:29.500 --> 00:51:32.280
which was commissioned
by the then sector state,

00:51:32.280 --> 00:51:34.920
Jeremy Hunt in 2017.

00:51:34.920 --> 00:51:38.240
Initially, to look at
the care of 23 families,

00:51:38.240 --> 00:51:41.850
but expanded very, very
significantly over time.

00:51:41.850 --> 00:51:46.413
And the final report comments
on the care of 1,486 families,

00:51:48.240 --> 00:51:51.960
and a total of 1,592 incidents,

00:51:51.960 --> 00:51:55.760
some families sadly suffering
more than one incident.

00:51:55.760 --> 00:51:59.240
And indeed in one case, I
believe three incidents.

00:51:59.240 --> 00:52:03.423
The incidents are dated
between the period, largely,

00:52:04.312 --> 00:52:09.136
2000 to 2019, there are a
few either side of that.

00:52:09.136 --> 00:52:13.600
And initially there was a report
published by Donna Ockenden

00:52:13.600 --> 00:52:16.680
and her team, in December, 2020,

00:52:16.680 --> 00:52:20.510
which laid down seven
immediate and essential actions

00:52:20.510 --> 00:52:24.110
for national maternity
and 27 local actions,

00:52:24.110 --> 00:52:26.490
which we have been following up on.

00:52:26.490 --> 00:52:30.380
The final report was
published in March this year.

00:52:30.380 --> 00:52:34.950
It contains over 60
actions for the local trust

00:52:34.950 --> 00:52:38.110
and a number of immediate
and essential actions

00:52:38.110 --> 00:52:42.360
for national maternity, which
are themed into 15 areas.

00:52:42.360 --> 00:52:46.890
But, total some nearly a hundred actions.

00:52:46.890 --> 00:52:51.770
Cases reviewed by a
wide team of clinicians

00:52:51.770 --> 00:52:56.051
who've worked incredibly hard
to cover such huge numbers,

00:52:56.051 --> 00:53:01.051
have been categorised according
to the type of incident,

00:53:02.610 --> 00:53:05.660
the standard of care and the
standard of investigation

00:53:05.660 --> 00:53:08.680
in those 1,592 incidents.

00:53:08.680 --> 00:53:13.680
And the categories of severity
nought to three relate to

00:53:14.550 --> 00:53:16.491
either there were no concerns in care.

00:53:16.491 --> 00:53:19.170
There were minor concerns
that they didn't think

00:53:19.170 --> 00:53:23.790
impacted on the outcome or
in the case of category two,

00:53:23.790 --> 00:53:26.650
there were significant
concerns which they believe

00:53:26.650 --> 00:53:28.390
may have affected the outcome.

00:53:28.390 --> 00:53:30.500
And in the case of category three,

00:53:30.500 --> 00:53:33.010
there were major concerns which they felt

00:53:33.010 --> 00:53:36.490
did impact on the outcome for those cases.

00:53:36.490 --> 00:53:40.460
The numbers in each group were that 198

00:53:40.460 --> 00:53:43.589
had significant concerns
in the category two,

00:53:43.589 --> 00:53:48.210
and 106 cases had major
concerns in category three.

00:53:48.210 --> 00:53:52.210
Those constituting in
the two groups combined,

00:53:52.210 --> 00:53:54.232
the groups two and three,

00:53:54.232 --> 00:53:56.300
the significant and major concerns

00:53:56.300 --> 00:53:59.340
that nine related to maternal deaths,

00:53:59.340 --> 00:54:01.810
131 to stillbirth,

00:54:01.810 --> 00:54:04.100
70 to neonatal death,

00:54:04.100 --> 00:54:06.363
and 94 to brain injury.

00:54:07.370 --> 00:54:09.960
Donna Ockenden and her
team are now offering

00:54:09.960 --> 00:54:12.155
one to one feedback to
some of those families,

00:54:12.155 --> 00:54:14.130
particularly, in category three

00:54:14.130 --> 00:54:16.340
and has written to other families.

00:54:16.340 --> 00:54:20.806
And a programme of supporters
in place for those families.

00:54:20.806 --> 00:54:25.174
We maintain contact with
Donna Ockenden and the trust

00:54:25.174 --> 00:54:29.580
to ensure that they have
all the support they need.

00:54:29.580 --> 00:54:30.413
Thank you.

00:54:31.870 --> 00:54:33.370
- Sarah-Jane, can I bring you.

00:54:34.560 --> 00:54:36.610
- Yeah, thank you, Ruth and
good afternoon, everybody.

00:54:36.610 --> 00:54:38.210
So my name's Sarah-Jane Marsh,

00:54:38.210 --> 00:54:40.600
and I chair the NHS England

00:54:40.600 --> 00:54:43.683
and NHS improvement
maternity transformation

00:54:43.683 --> 00:54:44.566
programme board.

00:54:44.566 --> 00:54:47.364
I just wanted to build on
what Ruth said a little bit

00:54:47.364 --> 00:54:50.320
about the impact of
Donna Ockenden's report.

00:54:50.320 --> 00:54:53.090
Obviously Ruth sort of fully acknowledged

00:54:53.090 --> 00:54:54.350
the impact on the families.

00:54:54.350 --> 00:54:56.360
This has also had a really profound impact

00:54:56.360 --> 00:54:58.970
on people delivering
maternity services as well.

00:54:58.970 --> 00:55:02.600
Everybody has read this
report with at times horror,

00:55:02.600 --> 00:55:05.670
shame, sorrow, for what they've seen.

00:55:05.670 --> 00:55:08.190
And I think a really mature recognition

00:55:08.190 --> 00:55:11.347
that whilst there is quite a
unique set of circumstances

00:55:11.347 --> 00:55:14.190
coming together at Shrewsbury and Telford.

00:55:14.190 --> 00:55:15.595
The reports of this report,

00:55:15.595 --> 00:55:18.770
that lots of people working
in maternity services

00:55:18.770 --> 00:55:22.617
recognise in part from their services too,

00:55:22.617 --> 00:55:25.035
and this isn't about
changes that we need to make

00:55:25.035 --> 00:55:26.950
at Shrewsbury and Telford.

00:55:26.950 --> 00:55:30.140
This is about changes that
need to be made right across

00:55:30.140 --> 00:55:33.430
maternity services, so that
we've got the best of each

00:55:33.430 --> 00:55:36.340
individual part of care all the time.

00:55:36.340 --> 00:55:38.820
So I think we've been
really so, yeah, I mean,

00:55:38.820 --> 00:55:41.238
all the leaders have come together,

00:55:41.238 --> 00:55:42.760
midwifery obstetric leaders and said,

00:55:42.760 --> 00:55:44.313
what can we do?

00:55:44.313 --> 00:55:47.200
We need to make these services
the best that we could be.

00:55:47.200 --> 00:55:49.617
I think it's also difficult
because there's a real balancing

00:55:49.617 --> 00:55:52.720
act for us here because we
know we have made improvements

00:55:52.720 --> 00:55:54.880
across the board in maternity services.

00:55:54.880 --> 00:55:57.660
We know objectively that they are safer

00:55:57.660 --> 00:55:59.340
than they were five years ago.

00:55:59.340 --> 00:56:01.380
The statistics tell us that particularly

00:56:01.380 --> 00:56:03.866
around stillbirth and neonatal death,

00:56:03.866 --> 00:56:07.850
and we want to give the message
that our maternity services

00:56:07.850 --> 00:56:09.470
are safe whilst recognising
there are things

00:56:09.470 --> 00:56:11.700
we need to do to improve.

00:56:11.700 --> 00:56:13.370
So what have we been doing as a Board?

00:56:13.370 --> 00:56:14.850
Well, clearly as Aidan set out,

00:56:14.850 --> 00:56:18.060
it's really important that
we implement the immediate

00:56:18.060 --> 00:56:21.010
and essential actions that
Donna has set out in the report.

00:56:21.010 --> 00:56:22.930
But it's also really important

00:56:22.930 --> 00:56:25.750
that we look for those thematic areas

00:56:25.750 --> 00:56:29.790
that we need to ensure have
got national leadership.

00:56:29.790 --> 00:56:31.957
And so the three things
that we've been focusing.

00:56:31.957 --> 00:56:34.340
I'm going to ask now, each
of my colleagues in turn,

00:56:34.340 --> 00:56:35.897
just to talk about these.

00:56:35.897 --> 00:56:37.980
Are the board assurance and oversight,

00:56:37.980 --> 00:56:41.730
because often when things go wrong,

00:56:41.730 --> 00:56:44.300
we wonder where the board
and the board oversight

00:56:44.300 --> 00:56:46.970
was at the beginning of this.

00:56:46.970 --> 00:56:49.280
What data and information
were people looking at?

00:56:49.280 --> 00:56:52.051
How were they connecting with
their maternity services?

00:56:52.051 --> 00:56:55.940
Why did it take so long to
realise that there was a problem?

00:56:55.940 --> 00:56:58.496
So we have to strengthen
the board assurance

00:56:58.496 --> 00:56:59.720
and oversight on the ground.

00:56:59.720 --> 00:57:02.510
The board's responsibility at local level,

00:57:02.510 --> 00:57:04.597
it is that safeguard
of quality and safety.

00:57:04.597 --> 00:57:06.950
And we have to support the boards

00:57:06.950 --> 00:57:09.981
with the tools to be able to do that.

00:57:09.981 --> 00:57:12.016
So Jackie's going to talk about that.

00:57:12.016 --> 00:57:14.307
Then we're going to talk
about leadership and culture.

00:57:14.307 --> 00:57:15.972
We need the right leadership and culture

00:57:15.972 --> 00:57:16.805
in our maternity services.

00:57:16.805 --> 00:57:19.138
People need the time to be able
to do that leading as well.

00:57:19.138 --> 00:57:22.890
And then finally the people
and the infrastructure.

00:57:22.890 --> 00:57:25.340
Again, if you've not got the
right people in the right place

00:57:25.340 --> 00:57:28.040
at the right time, it
becomes very difficult

00:57:28.040 --> 00:57:31.100
to provide safe high quality
care because gaps emerge

00:57:31.100 --> 00:57:33.830
and make things more
difficult and more fraught.

00:57:33.830 --> 00:57:36.400
So we're just going to cover
each of those areas briefly,

00:57:36.400 --> 00:57:38.490
and chair, and then we will wrap up.

00:57:38.490 --> 00:57:41.058
So first of all, can I ask
Jackie to talk through please,

00:57:41.058 --> 00:57:43.650
the work that she's been
leading around the board,

00:57:43.650 --> 00:57:45.080
assurance and oversight.

00:57:45.080 --> 00:57:46.320
Thank you.

00:57:46.320 --> 00:57:47.160
- Thank you, Sarah-Jane.

00:57:47.160 --> 00:57:50.480
My name is Jacqueline Dunkley-Bent,
chief midwifery officer.

00:57:50.480 --> 00:57:55.480
The maternity Board assurance
encompasses many items.

00:57:55.760 --> 00:57:59.100
One of which is the assurance
tool that's been developed

00:57:59.100 --> 00:58:04.040
specifically, for trust
to help measure compliance

00:58:04.040 --> 00:58:08.000
against the Ockenden immediate
and essential actions,

00:58:08.000 --> 00:58:11.420
both in report one and in
the final Ockenden report.

00:58:11.420 --> 00:58:14.860
And this of course is
supported by an infrastructure

00:58:14.860 --> 00:58:18.330
of regional chief midwives,
one in each region,

00:58:18.330 --> 00:58:20.250
deputy regional chief midwife,

00:58:20.250 --> 00:58:22.500
and regional obstetricians,

00:58:22.500 --> 00:58:26.630
because it's a multidisciplinary
team that will help

00:58:26.630 --> 00:58:31.530
services to provide and
have a sense of assurance.

00:58:31.530 --> 00:58:32.680
The process of course,

00:58:32.680 --> 00:58:34.900
is strengthened by regional oversight

00:58:34.900 --> 00:58:38.406
and also integrated care systems,

00:58:38.406 --> 00:58:42.090
local maternity systems
that will also provide

00:58:42.090 --> 00:58:45.540
assurance in relation
to quality and safety.

00:58:45.540 --> 00:58:48.440
And this is further
strengthened, of course,

00:58:48.440 --> 00:58:51.300
by the perinatal surveillance,

00:58:51.300 --> 00:58:54.720
early intervention and
improvement support.

00:58:54.720 --> 00:58:59.570
Very much badged under early
concerns and surveillance.

00:58:59.570 --> 00:59:01.960
And that supports really organisations

00:59:01.960 --> 00:59:04.100
before they are challenged.

00:59:04.100 --> 00:59:06.990
What we're keen to do
is to focus in an area

00:59:06.990 --> 00:59:09.890
that we frequently refer
to as being upstream,

00:59:09.890 --> 00:59:13.510
supporting services before
they become challenged,

00:59:13.510 --> 00:59:18.170
but using early insights
from the surveillance model

00:59:18.170 --> 00:59:21.369
to identify areas that need support early,

00:59:21.369 --> 00:59:24.680
focusing upstream.

00:59:24.680 --> 00:59:27.540
And so in addition to that assurance,

00:59:27.540 --> 00:59:30.280
I personally, along with
colleagues, Matthew Jolly,

00:59:30.280 --> 00:59:32.580
national clinical director
who's going to speak next.

00:59:32.580 --> 00:59:35.101
And other colleagues, Sascha Wells-Munro.

00:59:35.101 --> 00:59:39.210
We have been visiting maternity services

00:59:39.210 --> 00:59:42.960
and more specifically
engaging with trust Boards.

00:59:42.960 --> 00:59:45.830
You have maternity within their portfolio.

00:59:45.830 --> 00:59:50.830
We have now completed 67
meetings with trust Boards.

00:59:51.760 --> 00:59:56.560
And the focus of the meeting
is really on safe, sustainable.

00:59:56.560 --> 00:59:57.730
It's really important,

00:59:57.730 --> 00:59:59.960
sustainable maternity care,

00:59:59.960 --> 01:00:02.987
safe, personal and
sustainable maternity care.

01:00:02.987 --> 01:00:05.560
And looking at the learning from the past

01:00:05.560 --> 01:00:08.700
in terms of what didn't go quite right,

01:00:08.700 --> 01:00:12.880
and what we can do within an
appreciative inquiry framework

01:00:12.880 --> 01:00:14.770
to make things right,

01:00:14.770 --> 01:00:18.300
based on previous reports and
also in more recent times,

01:00:18.300 --> 01:00:19.900
the Ockenden report.

01:00:19.900 --> 01:00:20.810
And finally,

01:00:20.810 --> 01:00:23.710
I think it would be remiss
of me not to mention

01:00:23.710 --> 01:00:27.555
the most significant
part of our assurance.

01:00:27.555 --> 01:00:30.180
And that would be in relation

01:00:30.180 --> 01:00:33.370
to maternity voices partnerships.

01:00:33.370 --> 01:00:38.370
We know the evidence tells
us that if we place women,

01:00:38.390 --> 01:00:41.210
birthing people, babies and their families

01:00:41.210 --> 01:00:44.720
at the centre, front and
centre of everything that we do

01:00:44.720 --> 01:00:47.650
and are proactive in co-creation,

01:00:47.650 --> 01:00:52.590
then we will be having assurance
about safe maternity care.

01:00:52.590 --> 01:00:55.095
So, we have maternity voices partnerships,

01:00:55.095 --> 01:00:59.810
and they are predicated on
the mantra of no decision

01:00:59.810 --> 01:01:01.660
about me without me.

01:01:01.660 --> 01:01:04.900
So co-creation is taken very seriously.

01:01:04.900 --> 01:01:06.320
And we're also very committed.

01:01:06.320 --> 01:01:09.470
It's really important
to us to have diversity

01:01:09.470 --> 01:01:13.050
within the maternity
voices partnership members.

01:01:13.050 --> 01:01:15.630
And we are supporting them to ensure

01:01:15.630 --> 01:01:19.380
that they are diverse and
represent the communities

01:01:19.380 --> 01:01:21.990
that they are representing.

01:01:21.990 --> 01:01:22.823
I'll stop there.

01:01:22.823 --> 01:01:23.993
Sarah-Jane, thank you.

01:01:25.030 --> 01:01:25.863
- Thanks Jackie.

01:01:25.863 --> 01:01:27.110
If we go to Matthew then is going to talk

01:01:27.110 --> 01:01:28.590
about leadership and culture.

01:01:28.590 --> 01:01:29.786
Thank you.

01:01:29.786 --> 01:01:31.210
- Thank you very much, Sarah-Jane.

01:01:31.210 --> 01:01:32.150
Yes, I'm Matthew Jolly,

01:01:32.150 --> 01:01:34.280
and as being national clinic director

01:01:34.280 --> 01:01:35.280
for maternity women's health,

01:01:35.280 --> 01:01:38.370
I'm also one or two national
maternity safety champions

01:01:38.370 --> 01:01:40.120
along with Jacqueline Dunkley-Bent.

01:01:41.380 --> 01:01:44.450
It's really clear that
leadership and culture

01:01:44.450 --> 01:01:47.242
are two really important
themes that have come out of

01:01:47.242 --> 01:01:48.140
at the Ockenden report.

01:01:48.140 --> 01:01:51.630
And there's definitely
room for improvement.

01:01:51.630 --> 01:01:54.110
But I will be doing a
disservice to our staff.

01:01:54.110 --> 01:01:56.640
If I didn't acknowledge
that we've also got areas

01:01:56.640 --> 01:01:59.030
of great leadership and fantastic culture

01:01:59.030 --> 01:02:00.260
in our services as well.

01:02:00.260 --> 01:02:03.510
And we wouldn't have
delivered the 25% reduction

01:02:03.510 --> 01:02:06.940
in stillbirth or the 30%
reduction neonatal deaths

01:02:06.940 --> 01:02:10.210
without their sustained effort.

01:02:10.210 --> 01:02:13.250
Nonetheless, it's not universally good.

01:02:13.250 --> 01:02:14.940
And that means it's not good enough,

01:02:14.940 --> 01:02:17.277
which means that we need
to do something about it.

01:02:17.277 --> 01:02:20.636
And we have two funded
initiatives in place

01:02:20.636 --> 01:02:23.870
to address these issues.

01:02:23.870 --> 01:02:25.369
The first is relation to leadership

01:02:25.369 --> 01:02:28.690
and the team who've been
helping me develop this

01:02:28.690 --> 01:02:30.023
have been working with,

01:02:30.023 --> 01:02:32.281
Sir Gorgon Messenger's work on leadership,

01:02:32.281 --> 01:02:35.230
and also with the NHS leadership academy.

01:02:35.230 --> 01:02:38.650
The first focus is on what
we call the quadrivalent.

01:02:38.650 --> 01:02:40.560
That's the multidisciplinary team

01:02:40.560 --> 01:02:42.520
that leads every maternity service,

01:02:42.520 --> 01:02:45.770
made up of the director of midwifery,

01:02:45.770 --> 01:02:48.520
the clinical director of
the lead from obstetrics,

01:02:48.520 --> 01:02:50.470
the lead operational manager,

01:02:50.470 --> 01:02:52.820
and also representative
of the neonatal team.

01:02:52.820 --> 01:02:54.790
We think it's really important
to have that perinatal

01:02:54.790 --> 01:02:57.070
approach to how we drive improvement.

01:02:57.070 --> 01:03:01.730
And so, there's work on going
ahead to develop that team,

01:03:01.730 --> 01:03:06.730
to make them a higher performing
team as we possibly can.

01:03:06.780 --> 01:03:08.640
And there's also work underway

01:03:08.640 --> 01:03:12.310
to develop our aspiring leaders

01:03:12.310 --> 01:03:16.980
so that we have a pipeline of leaders,

01:03:16.980 --> 01:03:20.520
rather than people who are
suddenly thrown into the post

01:03:20.520 --> 01:03:22.290
and have to learn on the job.

01:03:22.290 --> 01:03:24.863
We need to get proactive about this.

01:03:25.813 --> 01:03:29.730
The second funded area
of work is on our culture

01:03:29.730 --> 01:03:31.760
improvement programme.

01:03:31.760 --> 01:03:35.940
That involves a diagnostic
phase where we are using proven

01:03:35.940 --> 01:03:38.939
culture surveys to really
get under the skin of units.

01:03:38.939 --> 01:03:42.440
And are developing teams
to help with that debrief,

01:03:42.440 --> 01:03:47.440
to really get that recognition
and self knowledge of units

01:03:48.000 --> 01:03:49.440
to understand what's going well and

01:03:49.440 --> 01:03:52.820
what's going badly wrong with
the culture of their units.

01:03:52.820 --> 01:03:55.550
And then we've developed a
portfolio of evidence based

01:03:55.550 --> 01:03:58.900
intervention so that we can
develop a tailored solution

01:03:58.900 --> 01:04:01.930
for each unit to drive improvement

01:04:01.930 --> 01:04:04.010
and for them to own the problem

01:04:04.010 --> 01:04:05.610
and drive their own improvement.

01:04:07.360 --> 01:04:10.810
So those two interventions
will be on the process

01:04:10.810 --> 01:04:13.810
of starting and are funded over
the next two to three years.

01:04:14.770 --> 01:04:18.000
But I think it is important to acknowledge

01:04:18.000 --> 01:04:21.560
that we need to create
the right environment

01:04:21.560 --> 01:04:23.897
to drive improvement in
leadership and culture.

01:04:23.897 --> 01:04:28.897
And that means that we must
resource our leaders properly.

01:04:28.990 --> 01:04:31.440
We've seen our maternity
safety support programme,

01:04:31.440 --> 01:04:34.590
the huge variation in the time
that's given to leadership

01:04:34.590 --> 01:04:35.850
in different units.

01:04:35.850 --> 01:04:39.780
Where some units, a clinical
director has half a day a week

01:04:39.780 --> 01:04:42.350
to lead a really complex large team

01:04:42.350 --> 01:04:44.830
where other units they've
invested more in the leadership

01:04:44.830 --> 01:04:46.030
and you see the benefits.

01:04:46.030 --> 01:04:48.230
And the same we see in our workforce.

01:04:48.230 --> 01:04:50.920
Again, a recurring theme in
terms safety support programme

01:04:50.920 --> 01:04:55.050
is that overstretched
workforces start to crack

01:04:55.050 --> 01:04:59.010
under the strain and
culture suffers as a result.

01:04:59.010 --> 01:05:01.010
So on that note,

01:05:01.010 --> 01:05:04.850
I will hand over to Duncan
to talk about the next phase

01:05:04.850 --> 01:05:07.393
of our improvement plan.

01:05:08.780 --> 01:05:09.819
Thank you.

01:05:09.819 --> 01:05:10.855
- Thanks Matthew.

01:05:10.855 --> 01:05:12.270
And good afternoon, everybody.

01:05:12.270 --> 01:05:14.310
I'm Duncan and I'm the
deputy chief nursing officer

01:05:14.310 --> 01:05:17.390
for England and work with
Jackie and Matthew and Sarah,

01:05:17.390 --> 01:05:21.510
and the team on the maternity programme.

01:05:21.510 --> 01:05:23.635
I think it's fair to say that workforce

01:05:23.635 --> 01:05:26.610
is one of our biggest
challenges and critical

01:05:26.610 --> 01:05:31.000
to the success of all of the
programmes of improvement here.

01:05:31.000 --> 01:05:31.910
And just to,

01:05:31.910 --> 01:05:33.840
I think it's worth
Amanda at the beginning,

01:05:33.840 --> 01:05:36.730
talked about COVID and
the impact of COVID,

01:05:36.730 --> 01:05:40.170
but just to perhaps put a
bit of emphasis on the scale

01:05:40.170 --> 01:05:43.780
of the support that our workforce
provide every single day.

01:05:43.780 --> 01:05:46.530
So, every day during the pandemic,

01:05:46.530 --> 01:05:49.210
approximately 1600 babies were born

01:05:51.170 --> 01:05:52.580
during this period of time,

01:05:52.580 --> 01:05:55.880
which is the equivalent to
one birth every 54 seconds

01:05:55.880 --> 01:06:00.860
in the NHS in England and a midwife sees,

01:06:00.860 --> 01:06:05.485
first midwife appointment every 41 seconds

01:06:05.485 --> 01:06:06.770
in March last year.

01:06:06.770 --> 01:06:09.470
So that's the scale of,
just a bit of an example,

01:06:09.470 --> 01:06:12.230
the scale of why our
workforce is so important

01:06:12.230 --> 01:06:15.235
and the fact that we make sure
we have the right workforce,

01:06:15.235 --> 01:06:17.900
as others have said at the
right time in the right place.

01:06:17.900 --> 01:06:20.487
So, we're working really closely across

01:06:20.487 --> 01:06:22.940
with Health Education England,

01:06:22.940 --> 01:06:24.490
with colleagues the Department of Health

01:06:24.490 --> 01:06:27.290
and across NHS England,
and through our regions

01:06:27.290 --> 01:06:30.150
and our trusts and local maternity systems

01:06:30.150 --> 01:06:35.150
to work on increasing our workforce.

01:06:35.530 --> 01:06:39.100
So, the 95 million pounds
that was invested last year,

01:06:39.100 --> 01:06:42.100
significant proportion
of that was to invest

01:06:42.100 --> 01:06:43.750
in increasing our establishments,

01:06:43.750 --> 01:06:46.630
both for midwifery and for obstetricians.

01:06:46.630 --> 01:06:48.607
We put some funding into
invest in increasing

01:06:48.607 --> 01:06:51.320
the establishments by 1200 midwives

01:06:51.320 --> 01:06:52.570
and a hundred obstetricians.

01:06:52.570 --> 01:06:54.830
And what's really pleasing to see,

01:06:54.830 --> 01:06:58.330
and you'll see this in the
report is that local trusts

01:06:58.330 --> 01:07:00.410
have continued to invest themselves.

01:07:00.410 --> 01:07:03.110
So then the increase in establishments

01:07:03.110 --> 01:07:04.490
has gone further than that.

01:07:04.490 --> 01:07:09.490
So 1,692 increase in
establishment for midwives

01:07:10.010 --> 01:07:14.030
and 181 for obstetricians
and an increase in maternity

01:07:14.030 --> 01:07:16.390
support worker posts as well.

01:07:16.390 --> 01:07:19.005
But we know we've got
challenges around vacancies

01:07:19.005 --> 01:07:21.600
in terms of filling that establishment

01:07:21.600 --> 01:07:25.930
and also particular challenge
around leavers and turnover,

01:07:25.930 --> 01:07:29.773
which we've seen increase
following the pandemic.

01:07:30.990 --> 01:07:33.180
So that's why we're
investing really heavily

01:07:33.180 --> 01:07:34.330
in improving that.

01:07:34.330 --> 01:07:38.450
So we've issued out 8 million
pounds worth of funding

01:07:38.450 --> 01:07:42.027
to retention roles, particularly
focused on midwifery,

01:07:42.960 --> 01:07:46.310
which actually I was a
trust recently told me

01:07:46.310 --> 01:07:49.200
within the first couple of
months of that role going in,

01:07:49.200 --> 01:07:52.390
they had stopped three
midwives from leaving

01:07:52.390 --> 01:07:54.240
because of the work and
support that had gone in.

01:07:54.240 --> 01:07:58.270
So I think, making sure
that that scale and approach

01:07:58.270 --> 01:08:01.060
to retention continues,

01:08:01.060 --> 01:08:05.400
and we've obviously funded
out to 157 units that support.

01:08:05.400 --> 01:08:09.350
We're also investing, continuing
to invest in professional

01:08:09.350 --> 01:08:11.153
midwifery advocacy.

01:08:12.280 --> 01:08:15.200
So provide, we've got
800 of those now really

01:08:15.200 --> 01:08:17.610
that's providing restorative
clinical supervision

01:08:17.610 --> 01:08:19.693
to our midwives and support.

01:08:20.540 --> 01:08:23.810
And those support roles
also providing support

01:08:23.810 --> 01:08:25.390
to student midwives.

01:08:25.390 --> 01:08:28.630
And we should expect to see this year

01:08:28.630 --> 01:08:30.310
through the work that colleagues have done

01:08:30.310 --> 01:08:31.850
in Health Education England

01:08:31.850 --> 01:08:35.700
in increasing student midwifery places,

01:08:35.700 --> 01:08:39.120
increases of midwives start
to qualify from this year.

01:08:39.120 --> 01:08:41.454
So we should start to
see the impact of that.

01:08:41.454 --> 01:08:44.480
And building on the success
of the international

01:08:44.480 --> 01:08:47.640
recruitment programme for nurses,

01:08:47.640 --> 01:08:50.900
we have launched last year,
an international recruitment

01:08:50.900 --> 01:08:53.370
programme for midwives
is a short to medium term

01:08:53.370 --> 01:08:55.850
approach to increased midwifery numbers.

01:08:55.850 --> 01:09:00.550
And it's good to see that
actually we are now nearly

01:09:00.550 --> 01:09:04.000
at 500 recruited and at
various stages of starting.

01:09:04.000 --> 01:09:08.460
And I was in Sherwood Forest
Hospital, a few weeks ago,

01:09:08.460 --> 01:09:10.260
visiting them maternity in neonatal unit,

01:09:10.260 --> 01:09:13.920
and actually met with a
new international midwife.

01:09:13.920 --> 01:09:15.930
And also an international nurse,

01:09:15.930 --> 01:09:17.390
who's been working here for some time.

01:09:17.390 --> 01:09:20.010
Who's started the shortened
midwifery programme

01:09:20.010 --> 01:09:22.170
of which the places
have been made available

01:09:22.170 --> 01:09:24.330
through colleagues at Health
Education England this year,

01:09:24.330 --> 01:09:26.987
as well, which was really good to see.

01:09:27.970 --> 01:09:30.490
In terms of investment for this year,

01:09:30.490 --> 01:09:31.930
at the Board last time,

01:09:31.930 --> 01:09:34.212
there was a funding of 127 million pounds

01:09:34.212 --> 01:09:36.700
investment announced.

01:09:36.700 --> 01:09:39.370
And a lot of that is going into workforce

01:09:39.370 --> 01:09:41.210
and to pick up Matthew's
point a moment ago,

01:09:41.210 --> 01:09:42.840
really, to help with things such

01:09:44.172 --> 01:09:45.958
as time for clinical leadership,

01:09:45.958 --> 01:09:47.802
continuing to right
size our establishments

01:09:47.802 --> 01:09:51.720
and support to recruitment and retention,

01:09:51.720 --> 01:09:56.010
both for maternity services
and neonatal services.

01:09:56.010 --> 01:09:57.546
So you'll see in the report,

01:09:57.546 --> 01:09:59.837
we've got investment to go
into neonatal workforce.

01:09:59.837 --> 01:10:04.837
But then also capital to
support neonatal capacity.

01:10:05.920 --> 01:10:10.120
So, really great to see
we started to communicate

01:10:10.120 --> 01:10:11.730
those capital investments out,

01:10:11.730 --> 01:10:15.550
which should provide an additional 60 cots

01:10:15.550 --> 01:10:17.400
across our services in England,

01:10:17.400 --> 01:10:20.520
which will really support
improving outcomes,

01:10:20.520 --> 01:10:23.020
particularly for those
neonates that are born.

01:10:23.020 --> 01:10:26.080
Those babies that are born under 27 weeks

01:10:26.080 --> 01:10:28.110
and making sure that they
have access to the right unit

01:10:28.110 --> 01:10:30.300
at the right time in the right place.

01:10:30.300 --> 01:10:33.550
So I will hand back to Ruth, I think.

01:10:33.550 --> 01:10:38.100
- Richard, just one final comment for me.

01:10:38.100 --> 01:10:41.750
So we have a clear challenge ahead of us,

01:10:41.750 --> 01:10:43.890
to consistently deliver,

01:10:43.890 --> 01:10:46.470
safer, more personalised
care and maternity

01:10:46.470 --> 01:10:48.960
and neonatal services.

01:10:48.960 --> 01:10:52.460
We must challenge ourselves
to do what it takes

01:10:52.460 --> 01:10:55.730
to ensure that we improve the practise,

01:10:55.730 --> 01:10:57.420
the additional investment

01:10:57.420 --> 01:11:00.000
and crucially working together with women

01:11:00.000 --> 01:11:01.600
and their families.

01:11:01.600 --> 01:11:03.793
We have much more to do.

01:11:04.760 --> 01:11:06.520
So the further learning we've got

01:11:06.520 --> 01:11:09.340
from the independent investigations,

01:11:09.340 --> 01:11:11.590
listening to the voice
of women and families

01:11:11.590 --> 01:11:13.500
and listening to our staff,

01:11:13.500 --> 01:11:16.250
we will bring together
by the end of the year,

01:11:16.250 --> 01:11:18.270
a refresh plan.

01:11:18.270 --> 01:11:21.490
I want to therefore just end on two quotes

01:11:21.490 --> 01:11:23.900
from Donna Ockenden's report.

01:11:23.900 --> 01:11:26.120
On page 150,

01:11:26.120 --> 01:11:30.430
Donna says, "we have seen
so much excellence practise

01:11:30.430 --> 01:11:32.383
and a real desire to improve".

01:11:33.720 --> 01:11:36.160
She says a bit later on though,

01:11:36.160 --> 01:11:39.130
"as difficult decisions
loom about NHS funding

01:11:39.130 --> 01:11:44.000
post the COVID pandemic,
maternity services in England

01:11:44.000 --> 01:11:47.360
must not slip down the priority list".

01:11:47.360 --> 01:11:48.961
Thank you for listening to this.

01:11:48.961 --> 01:11:49.794
- Yeah, Ruth, thank you.

01:11:49.794 --> 01:11:50.677
I'm just going to react if I might,

01:11:50.677 --> 01:11:52.720
and I can see Wol has raised his hand.

01:11:52.720 --> 01:11:54.083
I just want to react.

01:11:56.050 --> 01:11:57.700
Thank you Ruth to you and your team

01:11:57.700 --> 01:12:02.700
for a pretty comprehensive
review post, the Ockenden report.

01:12:03.160 --> 01:12:06.547
I have to say coming in as an outsider,

01:12:06.547 --> 01:12:10.980
I founded a truly, truly
upsetting report, I mean,

01:12:10.980 --> 01:12:14.453
truly disturbing and upsetting.

01:12:16.784 --> 01:12:18.884
And part of the solution you are providing

01:12:19.819 --> 01:12:21.333
to us goes to that capacity theme.

01:12:23.063 --> 01:12:24.970
And I'll come back to that in a moment.

01:12:24.970 --> 01:12:28.130
But Jackie, I did think
the section you presented

01:12:28.130 --> 01:12:31.130
on assurance and in direct
engagement with the Boards

01:12:31.130 --> 01:12:32.780
and asking the Boards essentially

01:12:34.254 --> 01:12:35.280
to make sure they're very focused locally

01:12:35.280 --> 01:12:39.623
on their maternity provision,
I think was powerful.

01:12:41.530 --> 01:12:44.393
Matthew, I think the question
I would still ask you,

01:12:44.393 --> 01:12:47.530
I think I heard it coming through.

01:12:47.530 --> 01:12:51.203
But whether we are now a
sufficiently learning culture.

01:12:52.700 --> 01:12:54.210
And a listening and learning culture,

01:12:54.210 --> 01:12:55.287
I know we're going to put the mother

01:12:55.287 --> 01:12:59.273
and the mother stroke
patient who is a new mom

01:12:59.273 --> 01:13:01.497
right into the centre of it.

01:13:01.497 --> 01:13:04.313
And we're going to work
together co-create.

01:13:05.210 --> 01:13:09.497
But you think we are really
now a learning culture.

01:13:09.497 --> 01:13:12.850
And then, I'm pleased Ruth that,
because I was going to say,

01:13:12.850 --> 01:13:15.090
can you come back to the Board.

01:13:15.090 --> 01:13:17.920
Because actually one of the
reflections from the paper

01:13:18.858 --> 01:13:23.117
and it was in Duncan's,
very cogent presentation.

01:13:24.410 --> 01:13:27.340
Frankly, it's a scale of pickup

01:13:27.340 --> 01:13:30.943
that we've actually unleashed
in the last few months.

01:13:32.600 --> 01:13:35.170
So, national investment
of 95 million decreases

01:13:35.170 --> 01:13:38.210
the establishment in midwifery by 1200 FTE

01:13:39.330 --> 01:13:42.630
and obstetric roles by
a hundred FTE and so on.

01:13:42.630 --> 01:13:45.490
And then we're going out to recruit 450

01:13:46.360 --> 01:13:49.970
international recruitment
of midwives since October.

01:13:49.970 --> 01:13:52.683
I think it is, yes, October
21, joining the service.

01:13:55.710 --> 01:13:58.180
It's quite a lurching,
it feels to me recent.

01:13:58.180 --> 01:14:02.818
And so, I'm pleased to see
that that's our reaction

01:14:02.818 --> 01:14:05.780
to what looked like a as partly a capacity

01:14:05.780 --> 01:14:07.560
type issue, actually.

01:14:07.560 --> 01:14:11.610
But also I do note in Duncan's
section, the still turnover,

01:14:11.610 --> 01:14:13.880
the midwifery workforce remains a concern.

01:14:13.880 --> 01:14:15.960
So I think it'll be very telling,

01:14:15.960 --> 01:14:19.200
and it's a really important
request that you do come back

01:14:19.200 --> 01:14:22.220
to us and just reflect as
you talk about that refresh

01:14:22.220 --> 01:14:26.210
strategy on whether you now
believe with all those steps.

01:14:26.210 --> 01:14:29.777
We have a maternity service,

01:14:29.777 --> 01:14:33.810
which we really all confident in, I think.

01:14:33.810 --> 01:14:37.220
And that's not at all
to comment or criticise

01:14:37.220 --> 01:14:39.330
on the huge amount of
amazing work that gets done

01:14:39.330 --> 01:14:41.180
by so many people in this place.

01:14:41.180 --> 01:14:42.780
But stepping back from
it clearly Ockenden,

01:14:42.780 --> 01:14:46.330
which is where I started
was a truly upsetting,

01:14:46.330 --> 01:14:50.270
truly upsetting report to read.

01:14:50.270 --> 01:14:53.143
So they're more comments than
questions actually, I think.

01:14:53.143 --> 01:14:54.560
But Wol, do you want to come in?

01:14:54.560 --> 01:14:56.490
I think your hand was up.

01:14:56.490 --> 01:14:57.840
- Yeah.

01:14:57.840 --> 01:14:58.673
Thanks Richard.

01:14:58.673 --> 01:15:03.673
So I echo your comments
around the upsetting nature

01:15:04.130 --> 01:15:05.830
of the report.

01:15:05.830 --> 01:15:10.830
I've spent time speaking with
Sarah-Jane, Jackie, Duncan,

01:15:11.320 --> 01:15:13.023
and Ruth, offline on this issue.

01:15:13.978 --> 01:15:15.700
You know how troubled I've
been about this ever since

01:15:15.700 --> 01:15:18.740
I've been on the Board
and was first raised.

01:15:18.740 --> 01:15:21.160
And so please, please understand,
as I asked this question,

01:15:21.160 --> 01:15:23.670
the background and
context, which I'm asking.

01:15:23.670 --> 01:15:26.300
So leadership and culture is at the centre

01:15:27.206 --> 01:15:28.580
of this in my opinion.

01:15:28.580 --> 01:15:30.760
And yes, we can put resources in,

01:15:30.760 --> 01:15:35.180
and yes, all the oversight
we need and we want.

01:15:35.180 --> 01:15:36.640
But if the leadership culture isn't right,

01:15:36.640 --> 01:15:37.700
it ain't going to work.

01:15:37.700 --> 01:15:41.600
And so my challenge is, or
challenge and question is,

01:15:41.600 --> 01:15:43.940
are we thinking hard
enough about the sanction

01:15:45.610 --> 01:15:49.700
if people don't follow
the culture that we want?

01:15:49.700 --> 01:15:53.230
If there are a very, very,
very, very, very small,

01:15:53.230 --> 01:15:56.730
I mean, very small number of bad apples,

01:15:56.730 --> 01:15:58.010
what are we going to do about it?

01:15:58.010 --> 01:16:01.950
I mean, are we assured that
we're going to have some way

01:16:01.950 --> 01:16:03.700
of addressing that?

01:16:03.700 --> 01:16:06.160
Because that's the bit which
is kind of missing all this,

01:16:06.160 --> 01:16:09.512
which is lots of things we're
going to do which are good

01:16:09.512 --> 01:16:10.920
and looking and going well, hang on,

01:16:10.920 --> 01:16:12.690
the dark side of culture is people carry

01:16:12.690 --> 01:16:14.570
on behaving badly and you
undermine your culture.

01:16:14.570 --> 01:16:15.700
Fundamentally,

01:16:15.700 --> 01:16:18.240
if you don't do something about that.

01:16:18.240 --> 01:16:19.073
- So

01:16:19.073 --> 01:16:20.503
So Wol, can I, before you
get a response to that,

01:16:20.503 --> 01:16:22.830
because David Behan is also,

01:16:22.830 --> 01:16:24.690
David, do you want to
come in and then Ruth,

01:16:24.690 --> 01:16:26.810
if you want to direct the answers to that,

01:16:26.810 --> 01:16:29.337
whether it's from you or for Matthew

01:16:29.337 --> 01:16:31.837
or from Sarah-Jane or
others in the team or Aidan.

01:16:33.297 --> 01:16:35.390
But David, do you want
to ask your question

01:16:35.390 --> 01:16:37.387
or make your comment?

01:16:37.387 --> 01:16:38.760
- Thanks Richard.

01:16:38.760 --> 01:16:41.204
Ruth and colleagues,
thanks very much for that.

01:16:41.204 --> 01:16:43.603
It was very comprehensive.

01:16:47.442 --> 01:16:48.275
On Tuesday of this week,

01:16:48.275 --> 01:16:50.110
I chaired the Health
Education England Board,

01:16:50.110 --> 01:16:54.099
and we considered the education
and training implications

01:16:54.099 --> 01:16:57.170
from Donna Ockenden's report.

01:16:57.170 --> 01:17:00.130
And to assist us in that conversation,

01:17:00.130 --> 01:17:05.130
we took it the reflections
from a student midwife,

01:17:08.800 --> 01:17:11.080
a first year student midwife.

01:17:11.080 --> 01:17:16.080
And an ST6 OBS and gyne senior registrar.

01:17:18.900 --> 01:17:22.809
And both of those reflections
were incredibly interesting.

01:17:22.809 --> 01:17:26.150
Both in terms of this
issue about the resource

01:17:26.150 --> 01:17:30.120
that was the third priority, I
think that you've touched on.

01:17:30.120 --> 01:17:33.065
And the great variability that exists

01:17:33.065 --> 01:17:38.065
for students undertaking
their OBS and gyne specialism

01:17:38.500 --> 01:17:41.220
about whether they've got time to train

01:17:41.220 --> 01:17:43.150
or whether they're part of the rota

01:17:43.150 --> 01:17:45.630
and just striking the balance
between the time to train

01:17:45.630 --> 01:17:46.463
and the rota.

01:17:46.463 --> 01:17:48.770
And I think it's worth flagging that.

01:17:48.770 --> 01:17:52.090
Because I think this also
speaks to some of your points,

01:17:52.090 --> 01:17:55.917
Matthew, about the culture
that exists in organisations

01:17:55.917 --> 01:17:59.873
and the investment in
training the next generation.

01:18:00.750 --> 01:18:02.814
The midwifery student
was very interesting,

01:18:02.814 --> 01:18:03.900
because I was trying to ascertain

01:18:03.900 --> 01:18:05.733
how far are the curriculum,

01:18:06.778 --> 01:18:07.980
both for undergraduate medicine,

01:18:07.980 --> 01:18:12.980
but also midwifery
degrees, change to reflect

01:18:14.400 --> 01:18:17.925
both Bill Kirkup's first
reports on university hospital,

01:18:17.925 --> 01:18:22.340
Morcambe Bay, but then
subsequently Ockenden one

01:18:22.340 --> 01:18:23.710
and Ockenden two.

01:18:23.710 --> 01:18:26.840
And it was very interesting
because there was a sense

01:18:26.840 --> 01:18:29.773
that it had influenced the curriculum.

01:18:30.860 --> 01:18:33.640
But what came out from speaking
to some of the midwifery

01:18:33.640 --> 01:18:36.230
students was the sheer variation

01:18:36.230 --> 01:18:40.430
in just how much practise
time practise education

01:18:40.430 --> 01:18:41.770
was actually going on.

01:18:41.770 --> 01:18:42.920
These are not my words.

01:18:42.920 --> 01:18:44.770
These are the words of students.

01:18:44.770 --> 01:18:49.770
And I think how we amidst
the resource constraints

01:18:50.140 --> 01:18:54.624
ensure that we are protecting
student time for education.

01:18:54.624 --> 01:18:56.370
I think is absolutely critical.

01:18:56.370 --> 01:18:59.350
Because that's where
culture begins to define

01:18:59.350 --> 01:19:02.200
for our future workforce, it strikes me.

01:19:02.200 --> 01:19:05.190
And where I would go with that, Richard,

01:19:05.190 --> 01:19:07.630
if I'm there is Matthew,

01:19:07.630 --> 01:19:10.110
I wonder whether I could
encourage some thinking

01:19:10.110 --> 01:19:12.550
as you're beginning to
mature the work on leadership

01:19:12.550 --> 01:19:14.783
to think about practise leadership.

01:19:15.700 --> 01:19:18.550
So when the senior people are not around,

01:19:18.550 --> 01:19:22.150
the people leading the shift
can often define the culture

01:19:22.150 --> 01:19:26.510
and the experience of new
workers in particular.

01:19:26.510 --> 01:19:29.260
So the confidence to
ask a senior colleague,

01:19:29.260 --> 01:19:30.550
how do I deal with this?

01:19:30.550 --> 01:19:33.184
If you are dealing with
it for the first time,

01:19:33.184 --> 01:19:35.641
strikes me as being a
really important part

01:19:35.641 --> 01:19:38.180
of Richard's question about,

01:19:38.180 --> 01:19:41.200
are we still a learning
culture and are we developing

01:19:41.200 --> 01:19:43.129
that learning culture?

01:19:43.129 --> 01:19:45.240
So that was the first point.

01:19:45.240 --> 01:19:48.450
And the second point, if may, Richard,

01:19:48.450 --> 01:19:52.690
I was really interested to
look at the annexes route

01:19:52.690 --> 01:19:54.270
that you'd provided to the report

01:19:54.270 --> 01:19:56.783
where you'd taken us
through region by region.

01:19:57.640 --> 01:20:02.640
And what struck me was the consistency

01:20:02.750 --> 01:20:06.530
with which risk assessments
and informed consent.

01:20:06.530 --> 01:20:10.020
They were more standards
on every single region

01:20:10.020 --> 01:20:10.870
of all the regions.

01:20:10.870 --> 01:20:13.630
And I just wondered
whether that also spoke

01:20:13.630 --> 01:20:17.440
to Jackie's point about over
searching assurance as well.

01:20:17.440 --> 01:20:18.950
And it partly plays to the clinical

01:20:18.950 --> 01:20:20.310
because it's clinical risk assessment

01:20:20.310 --> 01:20:22.600
that are actually being
talked about as well.

01:20:22.600 --> 01:20:24.470
And I would've thought that
clinical risk assessment

01:20:24.470 --> 01:20:28.120
was just a critical part
of ensuring the quality

01:20:28.120 --> 01:20:29.590
and safety of people.

01:20:29.590 --> 01:20:32.010
It was the one that flashed most amber

01:20:32.010 --> 01:20:33.883
across most of the regions.

01:20:35.480 --> 01:20:37.420
And I was just fascinated by that,

01:20:37.420 --> 01:20:39.450
and I'd be really interested to understand

01:20:39.450 --> 01:20:40.580
a little bit more about that.

01:20:40.580 --> 01:20:43.830
So a strategic point and a
technical thankfully should.

01:20:43.830 --> 01:20:44.860
- So Ruth, do you want to coordinate,

01:20:44.860 --> 01:20:47.875
who do you want to draw
in whomsoever you want

01:20:47.875 --> 01:20:50.060
for your team to have a
crack at those questions?

01:20:50.060 --> 01:20:51.980
Wol's question and then David's.

01:20:51.980 --> 01:20:53.567
- Absolutely, thank you.

01:20:54.493 --> 01:20:57.440
I'm going to ask Matthew
and Jackie as our Board's

01:20:58.640 --> 01:21:00.880
safety champions to have the first go.

01:21:00.880 --> 01:21:01.946
Thank you.

01:21:01.946 --> 01:21:02.779
Matthew.

01:21:02.779 --> 01:21:03.612
- Great.

01:21:03.612 --> 01:21:05.120
Thank you for all those questions.

01:21:05.120 --> 01:21:09.110
Richards, come to your question
or challenge, first of all,

01:21:09.110 --> 01:21:12.790
about whether we are more
of a learning organisation.

01:21:12.790 --> 01:21:16.620
We've seen huge changes since the launch

01:21:16.620 --> 01:21:18.970
of the maternity transformation
programme and our capability

01:21:18.970 --> 01:21:20.770
to be a learning organisation.

01:21:20.770 --> 01:21:23.879
So, we now have our prenatal
mortality review tool

01:21:23.879 --> 01:21:27.260
and also the investigations from HSIB,

01:21:27.260 --> 01:21:29.600
which involve parents
in the investigations.

01:21:29.600 --> 01:21:31.690
Which I think is one of
the most important aspects

01:21:31.690 --> 01:21:34.100
of how we've become a
learning organisation.

01:21:34.100 --> 01:21:39.100
So having the parents
perspective about what happened,

01:21:39.190 --> 01:21:40.380
what their experience was,

01:21:40.380 --> 01:21:42.390
why it went wrong through their eyes,

01:21:42.390 --> 01:21:45.194
has really shone a light
on how we can improve

01:21:45.194 --> 01:21:47.200
our maternity services.

01:21:47.200 --> 01:21:49.310
So that's been really important.

01:21:49.310 --> 01:21:51.690
We've also developed our
maternity voices partnership.

01:21:51.690 --> 01:21:54.220
So we've got women who
are actively engaged

01:21:54.220 --> 01:21:57.300
and see what it's like to use the service

01:21:57.300 --> 01:22:00.320
feeding into our knowledge about services.

01:22:00.320 --> 01:22:02.210
We've transformed our data.

01:22:02.210 --> 01:22:04.363
We now have a national
maternity dashboard.

01:22:05.750 --> 01:22:10.180
And I think that helps us measure
the numbers, but actually,

01:22:10.180 --> 01:22:13.110
although that's been very much the NHS way

01:22:13.110 --> 01:22:14.600
of doing things in the past,

01:22:14.600 --> 01:22:17.090
I think the capability that
Jackie alluded to in terms

01:22:17.090 --> 01:22:20.660
of how we've developed our
ability to gather intelligence

01:22:20.660 --> 01:22:24.470
about services and hear from the students

01:22:24.470 --> 01:22:26.297
who are using our service
from service users,

01:22:26.297 --> 01:22:28.916
from other organisations that
interface with the services

01:22:28.916 --> 01:22:31.830
and shine different lights on our service,

01:22:31.830 --> 01:22:35.140
has been really important
and gather our intelligence.

01:22:35.140 --> 01:22:37.710
We've also got our embrace
confidential inquiries,

01:22:37.710 --> 01:22:39.820
which are truly world class.

01:22:39.820 --> 01:22:43.540
And again, engaged with
women and really deep dive

01:22:43.540 --> 01:22:46.630
into many of those most
serious losses to understand

01:22:46.630 --> 01:22:47.860
what went wrong.

01:22:47.860 --> 01:22:50.150
And they have a really good engagement

01:22:50.150 --> 01:22:53.010
with many charities, many service users,

01:22:53.010 --> 01:22:54.920
many other organisations.

01:22:54.920 --> 01:22:58.250
So I think we are in a
totally different place

01:22:58.250 --> 01:23:01.080
to where we were when the
maternity transformation programme

01:23:01.080 --> 01:23:04.280
started and our ability to
be a learning organisation.

01:23:04.280 --> 01:23:07.830
And we have a lot of ways
now of sharing that learning

01:23:07.830 --> 01:23:11.020
and turning the learning in implementing

01:23:11.020 --> 01:23:12.130
new ways of working.

01:23:12.130 --> 01:23:14.150
So it is all very well
having the learning,

01:23:14.150 --> 01:23:15.980
but you have to make the learning count

01:23:15.980 --> 01:23:18.240
and implement change as well.

01:23:18.240 --> 01:23:19.073
- Thank you.
- Thank you.

01:23:19.073 --> 01:23:22.103
- Can I just talk about two other things,

01:23:22.103 --> 01:23:22.960
very, very quickly, indeed.

01:23:22.960 --> 01:23:26.220
David, your challenge about
everyone being a leader

01:23:26.220 --> 01:23:27.900
in the service absolutely agree.

01:23:27.900 --> 01:23:30.270
And that triumph quadrant approach

01:23:30.270 --> 01:23:32.640
is mirrored the whole way down the team.

01:23:32.640 --> 01:23:35.630
It's the root cause of
some of the worst outcomes.

01:23:35.630 --> 01:23:37.060
And we are working.

01:23:37.060 --> 01:23:39.150
We have our prompt training
programmes where people

01:23:39.150 --> 01:23:41.060
who work together, trained together,

01:23:41.060 --> 01:23:43.640
and we are working with the
THIS institute in Cambridge,

01:23:43.640 --> 01:23:46.060
who again, world class in understanding

01:23:46.060 --> 01:23:47.290
how teams work together,

01:23:47.290 --> 01:23:49.337
how you escalate in terms of emergencies,

01:23:49.337 --> 01:23:54.337
how people in the acute moments
of a crisis offer learning,

01:23:55.210 --> 01:23:57.600
offer leadership and followership

01:23:57.600 --> 01:23:59.410
to work as an effective team together.

01:23:59.410 --> 01:24:01.573
And finally, Wol's point,

01:24:04.050 --> 01:24:07.710
I think the two sides to
my response to Wol's point,

01:24:07.710 --> 01:24:10.250
one, we have process in
place within the NHS to deal

01:24:10.250 --> 01:24:12.050
with bad apples.

01:24:12.050 --> 01:24:12.883
But,

01:24:13.780 --> 01:24:15.800
I think a question we
all have to ask ourselves

01:24:15.800 --> 01:24:17.733
is why did the apples go bad?

01:24:18.880 --> 01:24:21.243
What's the environment those apples

01:24:21.243 --> 01:24:22.710
were in that made them go bad?

01:24:22.710 --> 01:24:24.460
Because I think there's a responsibility

01:24:24.460 --> 01:24:28.170
for the whole organisation to make sure

01:24:28.170 --> 01:24:30.860
that that doesn't happen
in the first place.

01:24:30.860 --> 01:24:33.630
And I think it's about
the environment we create,

01:24:33.630 --> 01:24:37.183
the leadership we offer
that is really important.

01:24:37.183 --> 01:24:39.900
Because the people who
go into midwifery nursing

01:24:39.900 --> 01:24:40.793
and medicine,

01:24:41.819 --> 01:24:44.620
the percentage of bad
apples at the beginning

01:24:44.620 --> 01:24:45.870
is very, very small.

01:24:45.870 --> 01:24:48.160
So yes, we do need to deal with people

01:24:48.160 --> 01:24:50.580
when they won't change and won't improve,

01:24:50.580 --> 01:24:53.070
but we have to shine a
spotlight on ourselves

01:24:53.070 --> 01:24:55.130
and understand our responsibility as well.

01:24:55.130 --> 01:24:56.630
Thank you.

01:24:56.630 --> 01:24:57.620
- Ruth.

01:24:57.620 --> 01:25:00.680
- Jackie, can you just
comment on the students

01:25:00.680 --> 01:25:02.560
that David raised.

01:25:02.560 --> 01:25:03.430
- And Jackie, and then after that,

01:25:03.430 --> 01:25:04.847
I think I am going to move us on actually,

01:25:04.847 --> 01:25:07.090
'cause I'm otherwise going
to get a very bad reputation

01:25:07.090 --> 01:25:09.133
for this timekeeping
is a really important,

01:25:09.133 --> 01:25:11.790
really important topic.

01:25:11.790 --> 01:25:14.380
- So I'll just respond to that
question very quickly then.

01:25:14.380 --> 01:25:16.660
I think it's a question that I've heard

01:25:17.530 --> 01:25:19.380
several times before.

01:25:19.380 --> 01:25:22.367
Student midwives have at
least 50% of their experience

01:25:22.367 --> 01:25:24.750
and their education programme

01:25:24.750 --> 01:25:26.316
within the clinical environment.

01:25:26.316 --> 01:25:29.073
And we are relying on
midwives who are supervisors

01:25:29.073 --> 01:25:32.090
for each of these students.

01:25:32.090 --> 01:25:33.070
Number one,

01:25:33.070 --> 01:25:37.490
to supervise them, to teach
them clinical skills, et cetera,

01:25:37.490 --> 01:25:39.860
to sign off their clinical
assessment documents.

01:25:39.860 --> 01:25:42.320
And that of course takes time.

01:25:42.320 --> 01:25:47.320
And within the context of the
pandemic, even pre pandemic,

01:25:47.700 --> 01:25:51.076
where we have staffing shortages
in various organisations,

01:25:51.076 --> 01:25:53.810
it does create a challenge.

01:25:53.810 --> 01:25:55.900
But, I think it's a point well made

01:25:55.900 --> 01:26:00.900
and I will discuss this with
our HEE and NMC colleagues.

01:26:01.070 --> 01:26:06.070
But certainly the challenge for
midwives who are supervising

01:26:06.670 --> 01:26:10.780
student midwives means that
they need space, time, capacity,

01:26:10.780 --> 01:26:13.623
thinking space to do that job well.

01:26:14.870 --> 01:26:15.703
- Jackie, thank you.

01:26:15.703 --> 01:26:18.600
Ruth, can I thank you so much.

01:26:18.600 --> 01:26:20.473
And all of the team,
I won't list them all.

01:26:20.473 --> 01:26:23.570
But thank you and all of the
team for the presentation.

01:26:23.570 --> 01:26:26.930
And I think we will get
you to come back towards,

01:26:26.930 --> 01:26:29.310
either, the end of this
year or early next year

01:26:29.310 --> 01:26:33.660
to give us an update and
assurance or more confidence

01:26:33.660 --> 01:26:35.160
or confidence, sorry,

01:26:35.160 --> 01:26:37.620
that we are taking the right steps.

01:26:37.620 --> 01:26:39.790
But thank you very much
for the presentation

01:26:39.790 --> 01:26:41.300
and the engagement.

01:26:41.300 --> 01:26:42.667
David, I'm going to move to you.

01:26:42.667 --> 01:26:45.210
And I think it's an update
and I think you'll be joined

01:26:45.210 --> 01:26:47.040
by Claire Murdoch and Tim Kendall.

01:26:47.040 --> 01:26:49.773
I think, it's an update on
children and young persons,

01:26:49.773 --> 01:26:54.690
mental health and adult
mental health backlog.

01:26:54.690 --> 01:26:55.523
- Yes, thank you, Richard.

01:26:55.523 --> 01:26:57.800
I'll personally be very brief
as is noted in the minutes

01:26:57.800 --> 01:26:59.050
to the last meeting,

01:26:59.050 --> 01:27:01.329
we said we would return to this
to have a fuller discussion

01:27:01.329 --> 01:27:03.530
on where we are with our
mental health service.

01:27:03.530 --> 01:27:06.840
I just think we've just noted
the how fantastic it is,

01:27:06.840 --> 01:27:08.660
when you get the experts in the room.

01:27:08.660 --> 01:27:11.440
So I would immediately hand
over to the experts so Claire,

01:27:11.440 --> 01:27:13.010
over to you to lead the discussion

01:27:13.010 --> 01:27:14.733
on the mental health services.

01:27:16.070 --> 01:27:17.430
- Thank you, thank you so much.

01:27:17.430 --> 01:27:20.740
And can I thank the Board
as well for making this time

01:27:20.740 --> 01:27:22.963
to think about mental health.

01:27:23.970 --> 01:27:25.620
Obviously, you've had the paper,

01:27:25.620 --> 01:27:28.721
so you will understand
that the headlines are.

01:27:28.721 --> 01:27:33.721
We had a plan, 2.3
billion pounds to invest

01:27:33.730 --> 01:27:36.464
in mental health by 23, 24,

01:27:36.464 --> 01:27:40.620
to see 2 million more people by that time

01:27:40.620 --> 01:27:43.620
across a whole range of services.

01:27:43.620 --> 01:27:45.380
So we had that in train already.

01:27:45.380 --> 01:27:49.850
And all I can say is, thank
goodness, and thank you,

01:27:49.850 --> 01:27:51.830
because we've ring fenced that money

01:27:51.830 --> 01:27:54.511
we've been able to do advanced planning.

01:27:54.511 --> 01:27:59.248
It was developed in a hugely
inclusive way if I go back.

01:27:59.248 --> 01:28:02.740
So we worked with third sector patients,

01:28:02.740 --> 01:28:05.530
professionals in the
fields, policy colleagues,

01:28:05.530 --> 01:28:06.800
campaign groups.

01:28:06.800 --> 01:28:10.530
So, although the NHS owned that plan

01:28:10.530 --> 01:28:15.030
and we have huge responsibility
for driving its delivery.

01:28:15.030 --> 01:28:18.753
I'm really proud to say, it's
a plan that belongs to others,

01:28:18.753 --> 01:28:20.543
not just us.

01:28:20.543 --> 01:28:22.420
And I also just want
to say a big thank you,

01:28:22.420 --> 01:28:23.500
to Amanda and Julian,

01:28:23.500 --> 01:28:27.221
because the money is the starting point.

01:28:27.221 --> 01:28:29.680
Its job begun, not job done.

01:28:29.680 --> 01:28:34.500
But we've had huge support
to make sure that every year

01:28:34.500 --> 01:28:39.230
the identified 2.3 billion or
share of it as we grow that,

01:28:39.230 --> 01:28:42.270
gets to the frontline faster and faster.

01:28:42.270 --> 01:28:46.520
And that is imperative, and
thank you for that support.

01:28:46.520 --> 01:28:48.480
Because, of course,

01:28:48.480 --> 01:28:52.040
the thing we need to do is
turn that money into delivery,

01:28:52.040 --> 01:28:55.140
which first and above
all takes our workforce,

01:28:55.140 --> 01:28:58.380
not only workforce, but mainly workforce.

01:28:58.380 --> 01:29:01.430
And so the planner has
given us security really

01:29:01.430 --> 01:29:03.460
to think five years in advance

01:29:03.460 --> 01:29:06.570
and to come up with long term plans.

01:29:06.570 --> 01:29:11.280
So the plan was really
vested in wide ownership,

01:29:11.280 --> 01:29:14.203
proper funding that we
had line of sight to,

01:29:15.250 --> 01:29:17.650
and good evidence base.

01:29:17.650 --> 01:29:19.163
And that's where we were.

01:29:19.163 --> 01:29:23.280
Then the pandemic struck and
the preexisting treatment gap

01:29:24.186 --> 01:29:28.015
that the plan was designed
to begin to reduce increased.

01:29:28.015 --> 01:29:33.015
And we all have our biggest
concerns about those people

01:29:33.640 --> 01:29:36.500
who need to access care
and support who struggle.

01:29:36.500 --> 01:29:39.249
And that's what really drives
us every day, not just us,

01:29:39.249 --> 01:29:42.310
the national team, but across the country.

01:29:42.310 --> 01:29:45.070
It's looking at really innovative ways

01:29:45.070 --> 01:29:46.870
to close that treatment gap.

01:29:46.870 --> 01:29:50.250
The plan isn't just one of
more, it's one of different.

01:29:50.250 --> 01:29:53.933
So as we heard with the
Ockenden item just now.

01:29:53.933 --> 01:29:57.220
Thinking about our partnerships,

01:29:57.220 --> 01:29:59.520
thinking about how we localise this,

01:29:59.520 --> 01:30:02.950
constantly looking at
equalities and inequalities.

01:30:02.950 --> 01:30:06.990
Constantly, really listening
to patients and their families

01:30:06.990 --> 01:30:09.530
has been absolutely key to
what we've done thus far

01:30:09.530 --> 01:30:11.670
and will do going forward.

01:30:11.670 --> 01:30:15.480
You've seen that there are
some things we did during COVID

01:30:15.480 --> 01:30:17.330
that we fast tracked.

01:30:17.330 --> 01:30:21.840
So we set up those 24/7
crisis lines a year early

01:30:21.840 --> 01:30:23.333
for adults, three years,

01:30:24.376 --> 01:30:25.209
early for children and young people.

01:30:25.209 --> 01:30:29.030
And we did that because it
feels like a long time ago,

01:30:29.030 --> 01:30:31.610
but when people couldn't
leave their homes,

01:30:31.610 --> 01:30:35.450
before we had the vaccine and
we were in major lockdown,

01:30:35.450 --> 01:30:39.320
it seemed to us really important
that mental health services

01:30:39.320 --> 01:30:43.040
opened 24/7 at least by phone

01:30:43.040 --> 01:30:45.830
so that people in crisis
or who were worried,

01:30:45.830 --> 01:30:46.870
could contact us.

01:30:46.870 --> 01:30:50.360
So, thank you to the teams who
did that across the country.

01:30:50.360 --> 01:30:55.013
We now get some 200,000 calls
a month into those lines.

01:30:55.920 --> 01:30:59.730
You see that we also set up

01:30:59.730 --> 01:31:01.220
and we hadn't planned to do this,

01:31:01.220 --> 01:31:02.770
this wasn't part of the plan,

01:31:02.770 --> 01:31:05.050
but we could see that the
mental health of staff

01:31:05.050 --> 01:31:08.450
was really in jeopardy, for
reasons we all understand.

01:31:08.450 --> 01:31:10.673
And the mental health set to move,

01:31:11.533 --> 01:31:13.720
to set up those 40 hubs
across the country,

01:31:13.720 --> 01:31:17.780
which are still functioning
and have seen so many staff.

01:31:17.780 --> 01:31:20.340
We also, and I just want to pull it out.

01:31:20.340 --> 01:31:22.580
It was really important to us,

01:31:22.580 --> 01:31:25.520
that seriously mentally ill
people or people with a serious

01:31:25.520 --> 01:31:28.290
mental illness, were not disadvantaged

01:31:28.290 --> 01:31:30.020
in how they accessed the vaccine

01:31:30.020 --> 01:31:32.090
when we began the vaccine rollout.

01:31:32.090 --> 01:31:34.500
There was huge concern actually

01:31:34.500 --> 01:31:37.080
that we would see the
inequality continuing.

01:31:37.080 --> 01:31:38.630
And I want to say huge, thank you.

01:31:38.630 --> 01:31:41.480
Because we work with
partners and colleagues

01:31:41.480 --> 01:31:44.180
and particularly third
sector and community groups.

01:31:44.180 --> 01:31:47.470
And I'm really pleased that people

01:31:47.470 --> 01:31:50.248
with the serious mental illness
during that whole period

01:31:50.248 --> 01:31:53.390
access vaccine roll out to the same level

01:31:53.390 --> 01:31:54.863
as the rest of the population.

01:31:54.863 --> 01:31:57.130
- Well, it's Richard, can I just,

01:31:57.130 --> 01:31:58.410
I interrupt a moment,

01:31:58.410 --> 01:32:01.900
because you should take the paper,

01:32:01.900 --> 01:32:04.410
which is a very good paper as read.

01:32:04.410 --> 01:32:05.483
- Okay, I'll move on.

01:32:07.030 --> 01:32:08.750
- There's a phrase that struck me,

01:32:08.750 --> 01:32:11.650
which is that workforce capacity
remains the biggest risk.

01:32:13.425 --> 01:32:15.840
I would like you or Tim to
give us some sense actually

01:32:15.840 --> 01:32:17.480
for how you are dealing with that.

01:32:17.480 --> 01:32:18.770
- Very well.

01:32:18.770 --> 01:32:19.780
I'll fast forward.

01:32:19.780 --> 01:32:22.590
One of the things that
happened during COVID,

01:32:22.590 --> 01:32:25.300
was we achieved one of the
five year forward views,

01:32:25.300 --> 01:32:28.310
which was the strategy before this one

01:32:28.310 --> 01:32:33.310
at targets to grow the
workforce by 19,000.

01:32:34.400 --> 01:32:35.810
And at the time that was set

01:32:35.810 --> 01:32:37.930
as part of the five year forward view.

01:32:37.930 --> 01:32:40.427
I don't think anyone thought
we'd really achieve it.

01:32:40.427 --> 01:32:41.470
Well, we did,

01:32:41.470 --> 01:32:44.080
but I think that people
knew that was a big ask.

01:32:44.080 --> 01:32:45.450
We did achieve that.

01:32:45.450 --> 01:32:47.590
So there's a lot to learn from what did

01:32:47.590 --> 01:32:50.590
we do there to achieve that milestone,

01:32:50.590 --> 01:32:51.970
which went nearly un-noticed

01:32:51.970 --> 01:32:53.930
because we are in the middle of COVID.

01:32:53.930 --> 01:32:56.903
That we are rolling
forward into our workforce,

01:32:58.160 --> 01:33:00.130
learning and actions now.

01:33:00.130 --> 01:33:02.660
The kinds of things that we are doing

01:33:02.660 --> 01:33:04.640
and that helped us then,

01:33:04.640 --> 01:33:06.890
definitely, we are designing new roles.

01:33:06.890 --> 01:33:10.340
So the mental health
support practitioner role,

01:33:10.340 --> 01:33:11.970
which we've seen in,

01:33:11.970 --> 01:33:14.850
IAPT by a different name in
the mental health support

01:33:14.850 --> 01:33:15.940
teams in schools.

01:33:15.940 --> 01:33:19.730
We've got 2000 of those
new roles in train now.

01:33:19.730 --> 01:33:23.460
And we're about to roll out
hundreds of new community

01:33:23.460 --> 01:33:26.310
transformation, mental health
support team practitioners.

01:33:26.310 --> 01:33:30.440
They year long posts,
you can earn and learn.

01:33:30.440 --> 01:33:32.540
We've written their evidence base.

01:33:32.540 --> 01:33:34.328
We've written curricular,

01:33:34.328 --> 01:33:37.230
and we are working with universities

01:33:37.230 --> 01:33:39.840
at least 13, if not more,

01:33:39.840 --> 01:33:43.340
who are both training and
trusts who are employing

01:33:43.340 --> 01:33:45.670
these people as they go
through their training.

01:33:45.670 --> 01:33:48.080
That's a really fast track way,

01:33:48.080 --> 01:33:51.430
given that it takes 15 years
to train a psychiatrist,

01:33:51.430 --> 01:33:54.050
three and a half years to
train a mental health nurse.

01:33:54.050 --> 01:33:57.490
We can bring these people
through bright values

01:33:57.490 --> 01:33:59.510
driven, talented,

01:33:59.510 --> 01:34:03.690
and it's a really fast way
of bringing new workforce

01:34:05.188 --> 01:34:06.670
into service.

01:34:06.670 --> 01:34:10.480
We're also of course,
looking at retention.

01:34:10.480 --> 01:34:13.020
And we're doing a lot
of work in that area.

01:34:13.020 --> 01:34:16.010
We are looking at training
the existing workforce.

01:34:16.010 --> 01:34:17.800
And for example, in CAMS,

01:34:17.800 --> 01:34:21.400
we train more than 2000
of our existing workforce

01:34:21.400 --> 01:34:24.650
in new interventions in recent years.

01:34:24.650 --> 01:34:27.430
- What's the size of
the existing workforce.

01:34:27.430 --> 01:34:30.210
- It's approximately, I
don't know what it is today,

01:34:30.210 --> 01:34:33.493
but it's approximately, 140,000.

01:34:34.750 --> 01:34:37.300
And we want to grow it
by a further 27,000.

01:34:37.300 --> 01:34:40.470
So it's going to be a
mixture of new roles,

01:34:40.470 --> 01:34:43.420
fast track new roles, retention,

01:34:43.420 --> 01:34:46.940
listening to what people
say about flexible working,

01:34:46.940 --> 01:34:49.380
exploiting digital capability.

01:34:49.380 --> 01:34:52.140
Because, we've learned during COVID

01:34:52.140 --> 01:34:55.660
that we can provide services
differently as well.

01:34:55.660 --> 01:34:58.900
So it can't just be about
working the workforce harder.

01:34:58.900 --> 01:34:59.860
We know that.

01:34:59.860 --> 01:35:02.980
But we can enable them
to work differently.

01:35:02.980 --> 01:35:06.440
So we are rolling out far
more digital capability,

01:35:06.440 --> 01:35:08.950
whether that's in the assessment place,

01:35:08.950 --> 01:35:12.530
how you book appointments in
the single clinical record

01:35:12.530 --> 01:35:15.550
space or actually therapy online.

01:35:15.550 --> 01:35:17.740
So we are doing a lot
there to enable people

01:35:17.740 --> 01:35:20.220
to work differently and more flexibly.

01:35:20.220 --> 01:35:22.820
And of course, we're working
with the Royal Colleges

01:35:22.820 --> 01:35:24.633
on how do we attract more people,

01:35:25.659 --> 01:35:28.980
the two psychiatry campaign, for example.

01:35:28.980 --> 01:35:33.080
How do we attract more
people into mental health?

01:35:33.080 --> 01:35:38.080
We do think that one of the
upsides of the pandemic,

01:35:39.204 --> 01:35:41.970
if you can say that one of the lessons

01:35:41.970 --> 01:35:44.040
that we need to bring from the pandemic

01:35:44.040 --> 01:35:47.250
is it's triggered a huge
interest in mental health.

01:35:47.250 --> 01:35:52.233
And we believe that is
already generating interest

01:35:52.233 --> 01:35:55.360
in those new roles that
I've talked to you about.

01:35:55.360 --> 01:35:56.193
I dunno, Tim, if.

01:35:56.193 --> 01:35:57.043
- Let me pause.

01:35:58.320 --> 01:36:00.530
Can I just bring Tim in
and then I'm see if people

01:36:00.530 --> 01:36:02.020
have comments, but it's a very good paper.

01:36:02.020 --> 01:36:04.160
And thank you for the update.

01:36:04.160 --> 01:36:07.990
Tim, any points you want
to particularly emphasise

01:36:07.990 --> 01:36:08.823
from the paper.

01:36:11.150 --> 01:36:12.100
- You on mute, Tim.

01:36:14.830 --> 01:36:17.453
- Should wear it on my
t-shirt, you're mute.

01:36:18.360 --> 01:36:21.540
No, the only thing I want
to add is that the, I mean,

01:36:21.540 --> 01:36:26.210
Claire said it about designing new roles.

01:36:26.210 --> 01:36:28.083
The way that we're doing that,

01:36:28.083 --> 01:36:30.580
is that we're taking the NICE guidelines

01:36:30.580 --> 01:36:33.850
for that particular area of practise.

01:36:33.850 --> 01:36:38.220
And we're extracting the
competencies of people

01:36:38.220 --> 01:36:41.730
who were involved in those trials,

01:36:41.730 --> 01:36:44.140
turning those into curricular,

01:36:44.140 --> 01:36:46.800
and then training people up to do them.

01:36:46.800 --> 01:36:49.120
So there are about as
close to the evidence base

01:36:49.120 --> 01:36:50.920
as you could get.

01:36:50.920 --> 01:36:55.650
And we've done that in
treatment of people with anxiety

01:36:55.650 --> 01:36:57.496
and depression and all that.

01:36:57.496 --> 01:37:01.470
We've done it for the
mental health support teams

01:37:01.470 --> 01:37:02.810
in schools.

01:37:02.810 --> 01:37:06.480
I did the systematic
review that was behind

01:37:06.480 --> 01:37:10.310
the interventions that
were training people to do.

01:37:10.310 --> 01:37:14.758
And we want to do that across
every part of mental health.

01:37:14.758 --> 01:37:19.758
And we're just at the moment
trying to appoint a national

01:37:19.820 --> 01:37:23.540
clinical advisor who will
head that process up,

01:37:23.540 --> 01:37:27.363
and sort of colonise the whole
of mental health, if we can.

01:37:29.010 --> 01:37:29.860
- Thank you, Tim.

01:37:31.942 --> 01:37:34.349
One more minute, Claire,

01:37:34.349 --> 01:37:35.530
- Just on the workforce front.

01:37:35.530 --> 01:37:38.110
But something that COVID really taught us,

01:37:38.110 --> 01:37:40.420
was in addition to our own workforce.

01:37:40.420 --> 01:37:44.020
If we can align more closely
with workforce in primary care

01:37:44.020 --> 01:37:47.160
in the acute sector so in the NHS family.

01:37:47.160 --> 01:37:50.270
But also actually third sector colleagues,

01:37:50.270 --> 01:37:52.870
we are doing an awful
lot of work with them,

01:37:52.870 --> 01:37:56.020
about either parts of the
pathway they can provide

01:37:56.020 --> 01:37:58.200
which helps with the workforce pressure,

01:37:58.200 --> 01:38:02.490
the backlog and treatment
and access and support.

01:38:02.490 --> 01:38:05.530
But also, developing new roles that can go

01:38:05.530 --> 01:38:09.120
across third sector, social
care and into health.

01:38:09.120 --> 01:38:11.567
So there's been lots of
innovation there as well.

01:38:11.567 --> 01:38:14.954
And I would like to credit
our third sector partners

01:38:14.954 --> 01:38:17.666
for some terrific work
in that area as well.

01:38:17.666 --> 01:38:20.010
Which is both about culture change,

01:38:20.010 --> 01:38:25.010
but also making more of available
workforce across sectors.

01:38:25.330 --> 01:38:26.163
- Very good.

01:38:26.163 --> 01:38:28.740
I'm going to thank you there just to say,

01:38:28.740 --> 01:38:31.672
I think it's a very good update
and it's a really important

01:38:31.672 --> 01:38:36.490
area of focus, first one of the
things that I was in a prior

01:38:36.490 --> 01:38:40.050
life when sharing one of the
opportunity areas in Hastings

01:38:40.050 --> 01:38:42.290
was to actually have mental health

01:38:42.290 --> 01:38:44.980
as part of the educational intervention

01:38:44.980 --> 01:38:46.940
into the schools in Hastings.

01:38:46.940 --> 01:38:48.380
And it was a very successful part.

01:38:48.380 --> 01:38:49.960
It was a very successful,

01:38:49.960 --> 01:38:54.300
but I mean, a very strongly
delivering partnership,

01:38:54.300 --> 01:38:56.370
key workers into individual schools,

01:38:56.370 --> 01:38:57.517
helping those more vulnerable families

01:38:57.517 --> 01:38:59.140
and more vulnerable people.

01:38:59.140 --> 01:39:01.330
But Claire and Tim, can I thank you.

01:39:01.330 --> 01:39:03.500
I'm going to need to move this on,

01:39:03.500 --> 01:39:05.440
but thank you very much for
what was a very clear paper

01:39:05.440 --> 01:39:08.300
and a very good update
and hugely appreciated.

01:39:08.300 --> 01:39:10.302
So thank you very much.

01:39:10.302 --> 01:39:11.963
I'm going to move us on.

01:39:13.150 --> 01:39:14.670
Amanda, can I ask you to introduce,

01:39:14.670 --> 01:39:16.420
I mean, Claire, I can
see you, I have seen.

01:39:16.420 --> 01:39:19.020
Yes, I can see Claire amongst
the celebrity squares.

01:39:20.212 --> 01:39:22.380
And Amanda, I think you
want to introduce this item,

01:39:22.380 --> 01:39:23.877
don't you.

01:39:23.877 --> 01:39:24.970
So let me just hand to you.

01:39:24.970 --> 01:39:27.260
- Thank you, Richard.

01:39:27.260 --> 01:39:29.550
So just by way of introduction,

01:39:29.550 --> 01:39:34.550
I asked Claire Fuller to undertake
an independent stock take

01:39:35.760 --> 01:39:38.870
back in November, 2021.

01:39:38.870 --> 01:39:40.080
Why?

01:39:40.080 --> 01:39:41.220
Because,

01:39:41.220 --> 01:39:44.860
the ICS's becoming statutory
entities represents.

01:39:44.860 --> 01:39:47.170
I think we would all absolutely
firmly agree with this.

01:39:47.170 --> 01:39:51.100
A moment of real change for the NHS.

01:39:51.100 --> 01:39:55.120
And a kind of radical overhaul
in the way that health

01:39:55.120 --> 01:39:58.800
and social care systems
are designed and delivered.

01:39:58.800 --> 01:40:03.310
So, my sense was that this
gives us a opportunity,

01:40:03.310 --> 01:40:07.090
Claire's work, to set out
a vision for primary care

01:40:07.090 --> 01:40:10.840
at the heart of communities
and at the heart of systems

01:40:10.840 --> 01:40:14.310
that both addresses the
concerns we've heard

01:40:14.310 --> 01:40:18.000
from patients about access from GPs,

01:40:18.000 --> 01:40:21.570
about workload and from the
wider kind of primary care

01:40:21.570 --> 01:40:26.570
community about recovery
reform sustainability.

01:40:26.860 --> 01:40:30.419
But also a vision that
builds on the opportunities

01:40:30.419 --> 01:40:35.419
from integration, from
investment and from innovation.

01:40:35.580 --> 01:40:38.570
Including in new roles,
digital technology,

01:40:38.570 --> 01:40:41.110
and new ways of working, particularly,

01:40:41.110 --> 01:40:44.990
that were put in place
around the vaccine programme.

01:40:44.990 --> 01:40:48.140
So a real moment for us
to get around primary care

01:40:48.140 --> 01:40:49.600
in a different way.

01:40:49.600 --> 01:40:51.290
Why did I ask Claire?

01:40:51.290 --> 01:40:52.770
Well, Claire's a GP.

01:40:52.770 --> 01:40:55.227
She's also an ICS chief executive.

01:40:55.227 --> 01:40:57.585
Claire has extensive experience

01:40:57.585 --> 01:41:00.670
in a whole range of different roles.

01:41:00.670 --> 01:41:03.946
So what she be brings
is massive credibility.

01:41:03.946 --> 01:41:07.400
But also enormous energy to this work.

01:41:07.400 --> 01:41:09.570
So, I'd like to thank her personally

01:41:09.570 --> 01:41:11.680
for what she has done
over the past few months.

01:41:11.680 --> 01:41:14.400
And although we are obviously
having this conversation

01:41:14.400 --> 01:41:16.270
in advance of publication,

01:41:16.270 --> 01:41:18.290
given the importance of the agenda,

01:41:18.290 --> 01:41:19.700
I think Richard,

01:41:19.700 --> 01:41:21.810
you and I both felt that it
would be helpful for Claire

01:41:21.810 --> 01:41:23.920
to give the Board an update,

01:41:23.920 --> 01:41:26.390
just reflecting the
importance of this work

01:41:26.390 --> 01:41:28.490
and the sort of timeliness of this moment,

01:41:28.490 --> 01:41:32.500
given ICBs, coming into being
in just a few weeks time.

01:41:32.500 --> 01:41:33.748
- Thank you.

01:41:33.748 --> 01:41:34.650
It's an essential
priority focus, actually.

01:41:34.650 --> 01:41:36.020
I absolutely agree Amanda.

01:41:36.020 --> 01:41:36.853
So Claire to you,

01:41:36.853 --> 01:41:41.000
I think for your reflections
on your stock take.

01:41:41.000 --> 01:41:43.860
- Thanks very much, indeed
Richard, and thank you, Amanda.

01:41:43.860 --> 01:41:46.880
So I must say that when Amanda rang me

01:41:46.880 --> 01:41:49.516
on the day of my ICS chief exec interview,

01:41:49.516 --> 01:41:50.605
and I took the call,

01:41:50.605 --> 01:41:54.640
I had no idea quite what I
was letting myself in for.

01:41:54.640 --> 01:41:57.810
So this has been an
extraordinary six months.

01:41:57.810 --> 01:41:59.908
So as Amanda said,

01:41:59.908 --> 01:42:01.270
the scope was to look at
integrated primary care.

01:42:01.270 --> 01:42:02.440
What's working in systems?

01:42:02.440 --> 01:42:05.400
Why it's working and how we
can accelerate the integration

01:42:05.400 --> 01:42:08.810
as we move forward as statutory bodies.

01:42:08.810 --> 01:42:12.527
So the minute Amanda announced
the announced the stock take,

01:42:12.527 --> 01:42:14.990
I have to say, my inbox just exploded.

01:42:14.990 --> 01:42:19.140
So, hundreds and thousands
of people in the end,

01:42:19.140 --> 01:42:20.440
actually making contact,

01:42:20.440 --> 01:42:23.510
wanting to tell me what was going well

01:42:23.510 --> 01:42:25.860
in their neighbourhoods, in their towns,

01:42:25.860 --> 01:42:28.300
in their systems for primary care.

01:42:28.300 --> 01:42:30.410
Things that they were incredibly proud of,

01:42:30.410 --> 01:42:33.090
and that they wanted to
share with other people.

01:42:33.090 --> 01:42:36.020
So other people could
do what they had done,

01:42:36.020 --> 01:42:38.190
because they had seen
real tangible benefits,

01:42:38.190 --> 01:42:40.444
not only for the population they served,

01:42:40.444 --> 01:42:44.810
but also for the people that
were working in their practises

01:42:44.810 --> 01:42:46.520
and across their PCNs.

01:42:46.520 --> 01:42:49.210
So we had this explosion of energy.

01:42:49.210 --> 01:42:53.377
We had a consensus and a joining
up of the ICS chief execs,

01:42:53.377 --> 01:42:57.090
coming together, all saying,
actually, we support this.

01:42:57.090 --> 01:42:59.753
We want to prioritise primary
care as we move forward.

01:42:59.753 --> 01:43:02.710
We understand that primary
care is the bedrock

01:43:02.710 --> 01:43:04.890
of the NHS and delivers as we all know 90%

01:43:04.890 --> 01:43:07.750
of all patient contacts every day.

01:43:07.750 --> 01:43:11.200
And then coupled with that,
we've also had national focus.

01:43:11.200 --> 01:43:12.450
So coming here today,

01:43:12.450 --> 01:43:15.630
but also at national leadership
events, a real focus.

01:43:15.630 --> 01:43:18.390
I think for the first
time in my, I'm very old,

01:43:18.390 --> 01:43:19.870
my very long career,

01:43:19.870 --> 01:43:23.230
a real national focus on a primary care.

01:43:23.230 --> 01:43:26.900
So I think we've had this
real lineup of energy,

01:43:26.900 --> 01:43:28.270
attention and focus.

01:43:28.270 --> 01:43:29.580
But what surprised me with,

01:43:29.580 --> 01:43:32.770
despite the number of people
that have come forward,

01:43:32.770 --> 01:43:35.080
actually the degree of consensus.

01:43:35.080 --> 01:43:38.970
So initially the consensus is
around, what's not working.

01:43:38.970 --> 01:43:41.170
And actually what people
have said is not working.

01:43:41.170 --> 01:43:42.560
And we've heard it here today,

01:43:42.560 --> 01:43:44.620
across many of the items already.

01:43:44.620 --> 01:43:48.150
Access, continuity, and prioritisation.

01:43:48.150 --> 01:43:50.160
But then what was even
more surprising actually,

01:43:50.160 --> 01:43:52.420
was not only was there consensus
about what's not working,

01:43:52.420 --> 01:43:55.580
but also an involving consensus
about what we need to do

01:43:55.580 --> 01:43:57.990
differently to make it better.

01:43:57.990 --> 01:44:00.257
So, we heard time and again,

01:44:00.257 --> 01:44:03.260
and we heard example after example

01:44:03.260 --> 01:44:05.410
of people that have transitioned

01:44:05.410 --> 01:44:08.360
from the traditional sort of PCN,

01:44:08.360 --> 01:44:10.910
the primary care network contract.

01:44:10.910 --> 01:44:14.180
And have already moved into
integrated neighbourhood teams.

01:44:14.180 --> 01:44:15.930
And what we've seen and
when you read the report,

01:44:15.930 --> 01:44:17.520
what you'll see,

01:44:17.520 --> 01:44:19.790
is a commitment to
moving towards integrated

01:44:19.790 --> 01:44:22.530
neighbourhood teams,
wrapped around practises.

01:44:22.530 --> 01:44:24.660
And a commitment to
delivering three types of care

01:44:24.660 --> 01:44:25.510
in different way.

01:44:26.721 --> 01:44:28.084
And again, the themes have come up

01:44:28.084 --> 01:44:29.216
throughout the meeting today,

01:44:29.216 --> 01:44:31.224
which is like's been
fascinating actually sitting in.

01:44:31.224 --> 01:44:32.509
So urgent care,

01:44:32.509 --> 01:44:33.370
we need to do urgent
care in a different way.

01:44:33.370 --> 01:44:36.690
And if you look so you
were talking earlier

01:44:36.690 --> 01:44:38.030
about the urgent care pressures.

01:44:38.030 --> 01:44:40.860
Will urgent care pressures start
don't they in primary care,

01:44:40.860 --> 01:44:42.010
they start in general practise.

01:44:42.010 --> 01:44:43.480
They start in people's homes.

01:44:43.480 --> 01:44:45.840
But I think we often forget
that actually the care

01:44:45.840 --> 01:44:47.970
that we can access from our own homes,

01:44:47.970 --> 01:44:52.210
is not just about A&amp;E, it's
not just about GP appointments.

01:44:52.210 --> 01:44:54.000
It is about going to
community pharmacists.

01:44:54.000 --> 01:44:55.620
It's what's available online.

01:44:55.620 --> 01:44:57.520
It's our sexual health services.

01:44:57.520 --> 01:45:00.320
It's our health visitors,
our school nurses.

01:45:00.320 --> 01:45:03.210
The amount of urgent
care that we can access

01:45:03.210 --> 01:45:06.590
is much broader than I think
we traditionally realise it is.

01:45:06.590 --> 01:45:08.960
And then if you look at how
people have started to organise

01:45:08.960 --> 01:45:12.443
themselves across their primary
care network footprints.

01:45:13.383 --> 01:45:14.390
You can see how they're bringing together

01:45:14.390 --> 01:45:17.640
all of that available resource
to deal with all the demand

01:45:17.640 --> 01:45:18.980
as it comes in in multiple ways,

01:45:18.980 --> 01:45:20.940
as it comes in with people
walking into practise,

01:45:20.940 --> 01:45:23.750
people phoning up, or people
making digital contacts.

01:45:23.750 --> 01:45:27.280
So if you look at the very
fabulous Pramit Patel John Young

01:45:27.280 --> 01:45:30.510
in Greystone, who have
created a single team

01:45:30.510 --> 01:45:32.040
that deals with all the demand on the day

01:45:32.040 --> 01:45:36.040
led by 1 GP, working
with a team of people.

01:45:36.040 --> 01:45:38.579
It has revolutionised
how they manage demand

01:45:38.579 --> 01:45:40.570
and how they look after people

01:45:40.570 --> 01:45:43.036
who have got urgent care needs.

01:45:43.036 --> 01:45:45.290
So urgent care was
something that people agreed

01:45:45.290 --> 01:45:46.985
that we needed to do differently.

01:45:46.985 --> 01:45:48.980
One of the other bits is how we look after

01:45:48.980 --> 01:45:51.640
our most complex patients.

01:45:51.640 --> 01:45:53.370
So these will be the patients again,

01:45:53.370 --> 01:45:55.130
and we've talked about
many of these patients

01:45:55.130 --> 01:45:57.060
only when we were talking
about, again, urgent care

01:45:57.060 --> 01:45:59.670
and admission, avoidance
and discharge and flow.

01:45:59.670 --> 01:46:02.340
These are the patients
that keep ending up going

01:46:02.340 --> 01:46:04.630
into hospital and spending
longer in hospital

01:46:04.630 --> 01:46:07.200
and often having life changing events

01:46:07.200 --> 01:46:08.640
by being in hospital too long

01:46:08.640 --> 01:46:10.490
and being unable to return home.

01:46:10.490 --> 01:46:13.870
We need to wrap a team
around those people,

01:46:13.870 --> 01:46:15.990
increasing the skill
set that is available.

01:46:15.990 --> 01:46:19.340
So this isn't just about
1 GP working in isolation.

01:46:19.340 --> 01:46:21.805
This is about GPs working
with community services,

01:46:21.805 --> 01:46:24.330
working with secondary care consultants

01:46:24.330 --> 01:46:27.250
to give expert advice coming,
working with social care,

01:46:27.250 --> 01:46:29.070
creating a single team,

01:46:29.070 --> 01:46:31.780
working with a population
that we've identified

01:46:31.780 --> 01:46:34.560
as being our most vulnerable patients.

01:46:34.560 --> 01:46:35.770
And the third thing people said

01:46:35.770 --> 01:46:37.890
they want to do
differently was prevention.

01:46:37.890 --> 01:46:40.840
So we talk a lot about
prevention in the NHS,

01:46:40.840 --> 01:46:43.700
but have singularly I
think, failed to wholesale,

01:46:43.700 --> 01:46:44.720
do it differently.

01:46:44.720 --> 01:46:46.620
We started to do this over COVID.

01:46:46.620 --> 01:46:50.290
And you can see the real impact
that vaccine programme had.

01:46:50.290 --> 01:46:51.610
And the completely different way

01:46:51.610 --> 01:46:53.710
that we started to tackle
health inequalities,

01:46:53.710 --> 01:46:55.430
working with community champions.

01:46:55.430 --> 01:46:56.770
And as you travel around the country,

01:46:56.770 --> 01:46:59.596
if you see fantastic work going on,

01:46:59.596 --> 01:47:01.720
it's like the healthy
hive PCM that Andy Knox

01:47:01.720 --> 01:47:04.920
is working Morcambe Bay, like
Julian Oro down in Horley.

01:47:04.920 --> 01:47:07.143
They are working in their communities.

01:47:07.143 --> 01:47:10.990
They're working with people on
issues that matter to people

01:47:10.990 --> 01:47:13.810
and actually properly
addressing health inequalities

01:47:13.810 --> 01:47:15.940
in a way that will be sustainable.

01:47:15.940 --> 01:47:17.540
So when you read the report,

01:47:17.540 --> 01:47:20.720
I hope you will see
this real shift towards

01:47:20.720 --> 01:47:23.290
working in communities,

01:47:23.290 --> 01:47:26.570
in partnership with people
and organisations working

01:47:26.570 --> 01:47:27.910
for populations rather

01:47:27.910 --> 01:47:30.740
than for their individual organisation.

01:47:30.740 --> 01:47:33.840
But there is also a
request that comes through

01:47:33.840 --> 01:47:35.980
for more support around estates.

01:47:35.980 --> 01:47:38.930
Around work workforce, around
leadership and training.

01:47:38.930 --> 01:47:40.562
And particularly around data.

01:47:40.562 --> 01:47:41.513
And I think, again,

01:47:41.513 --> 01:47:42.346
all of those themes have come through

01:47:42.346 --> 01:47:44.500
in every conversation we've had today.

01:47:44.500 --> 01:47:46.450
So my conclusion is this,

01:47:46.450 --> 01:47:50.279
43 days until ICS has
become a proper thing

01:47:50.279 --> 01:47:52.880
before we become a statutory thing.

01:47:52.880 --> 01:47:55.280
And actually as I've gone
over the last six months,

01:47:55.280 --> 01:47:57.340
I think I've increasingly realised,

01:47:57.340 --> 01:48:01.770
we set out to sort out access
continuity and prioritisation.

01:48:01.770 --> 01:48:03.090
And I initially thought, well,

01:48:03.090 --> 01:48:04.690
you can't just do this
in general practise.

01:48:04.690 --> 01:48:06.650
This is about the whole
family of primary care

01:48:06.650 --> 01:48:08.550
that pharmacists, the optoms and dentists.

01:48:08.550 --> 01:48:11.630
But increasingly I realised,
it is bigger than primary care.

01:48:11.630 --> 01:48:14.570
And having listened to the
really good conversations

01:48:14.570 --> 01:48:17.660
I've gone on today, this is
actually about how ICSs do,

01:48:17.660 --> 01:48:19.020
how we do our business,

01:48:19.020 --> 01:48:22.450
and how we move from being
complex governance structures

01:48:22.450 --> 01:48:25.740
and actually into vehicles
that deliver real care

01:48:25.740 --> 01:48:27.910
for people in their communities.

01:48:27.910 --> 01:48:29.040
So if you're just indulge me Richard,

01:48:29.040 --> 01:48:30.620
I did have a list of thank yous, really.

01:48:30.620 --> 01:48:34.050
I've worked with an incredible
team who have worked so hard

01:48:34.050 --> 01:48:37.070
over the last six months
at ridiculous times of day,

01:48:37.070 --> 01:48:38.690
turning things around at ridiculous speed.

01:48:38.690 --> 01:48:39.790
So I did want to thank well.

01:48:39.790 --> 01:48:41.520
I want to thank Amanda
for asking me to do this,

01:48:41.520 --> 01:48:45.200
'cause I've have, well,
it's been an experience.

01:48:45.200 --> 01:48:47.500
But I have learnt more
than I thought I would.

01:48:49.131 --> 01:48:50.770
And it has just been an
extraordinary experience.

01:48:50.770 --> 01:48:52.080
But the team have been fantastic.

01:48:52.080 --> 01:48:54.930
The ICS chief execs, all coming together,

01:48:54.930 --> 01:48:57.340
uniting and prioritising
primary care was something

01:48:57.340 --> 01:48:59.100
I didn't think would ever happen.

01:48:59.100 --> 01:49:01.830
And then the national focus
we've also had on primary care.

01:49:01.830 --> 01:49:04.580
So, I will stop talking Richard.

01:49:04.580 --> 01:49:07.200
But thank you very much
for the time today.

01:49:07.200 --> 01:49:09.686
- Claire, that was
very, very good, indeed.

01:49:09.686 --> 01:49:10.519
Actually, thank you.

01:49:10.519 --> 01:49:11.710
And I look forward to getting,

01:49:11.710 --> 01:49:14.710
and seeing the final report at some stage.

01:49:14.710 --> 01:49:17.060
But you have shared some
of your emerging thinking

01:49:17.060 --> 01:49:18.190
for which I'm very grateful.

01:49:18.190 --> 01:49:22.750
So let me open it up to
any comments or questions,

01:49:22.750 --> 01:49:24.370
I'm looking for that.

01:49:24.370 --> 01:49:26.290
The hand signal to come up.

01:49:26.290 --> 01:49:27.823
Any comments or questions.

01:49:30.660 --> 01:49:32.200
Duncan, your hands up.

01:49:32.200 --> 01:49:33.033
Yeah, thank you.

01:49:33.033 --> 01:49:33.866
- Yeah, thanks.

01:49:33.866 --> 01:49:35.978
Thank Richard, and I just
really wanted to say,

01:49:35.978 --> 01:49:37.090
thank you to Claire.

01:49:37.090 --> 01:49:40.010
I met with Claire yesterday to
talk through this and really

01:49:40.010 --> 01:49:44.610
the recognition around the wider workforce

01:49:44.610 --> 01:49:48.290
within primary care,
particularly nursing roles,

01:49:48.290 --> 01:49:49.770
allied health professionals.

01:49:49.770 --> 01:49:54.260
And having spent some time with a practise

01:49:54.260 --> 01:49:57.670
in Southeast London recently.

01:49:57.670 --> 01:50:02.450
And really, the importance of
opportunities around advanced

01:50:02.450 --> 01:50:04.744
practise advanced clinical practise roles,

01:50:04.744 --> 01:50:08.340
particularly leadership roles for nurses

01:50:08.340 --> 01:50:10.540
and other allied health
professionals in primary care.

01:50:10.540 --> 01:50:13.500
And I'm really grateful
for Claire for recognising

01:50:13.500 --> 01:50:16.143
and supporting those elements as well.

01:50:19.600 --> 01:50:22.140
- Thank you very much, Duncan.

01:50:22.140 --> 01:50:26.130
I can see David and Tim Ferris.

01:50:26.130 --> 01:50:27.650
Yeah, David Behan.

01:50:27.650 --> 01:50:30.390
Do you want to ask your
questions or make your comments

01:50:30.390 --> 01:50:33.300
and then get Tim to do
the same, and then Claire,

01:50:33.300 --> 01:50:35.170
I'll get you to respond to those.

01:50:35.170 --> 01:50:36.794
So David.

01:50:36.794 --> 01:50:37.940
- Thanks, Richard.

01:50:37.940 --> 01:50:40.205
Claire, thank you very much for that.

01:50:40.205 --> 01:50:42.110
And your enthusiasm is infectious,

01:50:42.110 --> 01:50:43.913
so thank you for that as well.

01:50:44.885 --> 01:50:48.510
It' more a comment, Richard
actually, and Claire.

01:50:48.510 --> 01:50:51.690
One is, I really like the
framing around integrated

01:50:51.690 --> 01:50:54.940
neighbourhood teams and actually
beginning to map a vision

01:50:54.940 --> 01:50:57.540
for the future that
allows people particularly

01:50:57.540 --> 01:51:01.120
at a local level, to begin to
think in quite concrete terms

01:51:01.120 --> 01:51:04.250
about what some of these
quite abstract concepts,

01:51:04.250 --> 01:51:05.470
meaning practise.

01:51:05.470 --> 01:51:09.040
And I can see practitioners
locally really engaging

01:51:09.040 --> 01:51:13.140
with the narrative, whatever
organisation they work in.

01:51:13.140 --> 01:51:15.670
But I also thought your framing, Claire,

01:51:15.670 --> 01:51:19.753
from complex governance
structures to way of delivering.

01:51:20.628 --> 01:51:22.960
I had really encourages
to think as that as being

01:51:22.960 --> 01:51:25.160
part of the narrative that we develop

01:51:25.160 --> 01:51:26.910
when this report is published.

01:51:26.910 --> 01:51:30.500
Again, I think it moves from
what could be quite abstract

01:51:30.500 --> 01:51:33.870
policy into quite a focus on delivery.

01:51:33.870 --> 01:51:36.080
Because we'll be judged
by how this is delivered,

01:51:36.080 --> 01:51:38.322
not by the elegance of
the policy architecture.

01:51:38.322 --> 01:51:40.763
And I think keeping that
focus is going to be critical.

01:51:40.763 --> 01:51:45.763
So, I'd really encourage us to
continue to keep that as part

01:51:45.890 --> 01:51:47.160
of the narrative going forward.

01:51:47.160 --> 01:51:48.510
But thank you for the work.

01:51:50.080 --> 01:51:50.913
- Thanks David.

01:51:50.913 --> 01:51:51.746
Tim.

01:51:53.770 --> 01:51:54.603
- Thank you, chair.

01:51:54.603 --> 01:51:56.750
And Claire, thank you.

01:51:56.750 --> 01:51:58.073
It's been a pleasure.

01:51:59.081 --> 01:52:01.950
I've been a participant in the process

01:52:01.950 --> 01:52:06.530
and the enthusiasm of
the team around this.

01:52:06.530 --> 01:52:10.401
And because the prize is
so big and so important

01:52:10.401 --> 01:52:14.210
in this case, I want to just
reflect for just a second,

01:52:14.210 --> 01:52:18.150
if I might, on my own journey
as a primary care physician

01:52:18.150 --> 01:52:21.333
in a practise that had to
undergo a transformation.

01:52:23.078 --> 01:52:26.440
And it actually is very similar
to the story that I heard

01:52:26.440 --> 01:52:30.380
of Masoodd Nazir at Hall Green practise,

01:52:30.380 --> 01:52:32.210
who I visited a couple months ago

01:52:32.210 --> 01:52:34.027
and told his story of transformation.

01:52:34.027 --> 01:52:36.220
And I want to emphasise
a couple of points.

01:52:36.220 --> 01:52:37.053
And that is,

01:52:38.810 --> 01:52:40.253
practise transformation,

01:52:41.370 --> 01:52:44.460
changing the way you work
and the way you have worked

01:52:44.460 --> 01:52:46.680
every day for years.

01:52:46.680 --> 01:52:51.547
And it is a really,
really hard thing to do.

01:52:51.547 --> 01:52:56.140
And I just want to emphasise
that we need to support

01:52:56.140 --> 01:52:59.540
the community of GPs
and primary caregivers

01:52:59.540 --> 01:53:03.330
through a very difficult process.

01:53:03.330 --> 01:53:08.330
The way I like to think
about it as a physician

01:53:08.430 --> 01:53:12.627
is that I want physicians
to be spending 99.9%

01:53:13.550 --> 01:53:18.420
of their brain energy,
thinking about their patients

01:53:18.420 --> 01:53:21.770
and not about the processes around them,

01:53:21.770 --> 01:53:24.040
that enable them to deliver services.

01:53:24.040 --> 01:53:27.681
And when you engage in a change process

01:53:27.681 --> 01:53:29.120
in the delivery of services,

01:53:29.120 --> 01:53:31.830
you are not spending 99.9% of your time

01:53:31.830 --> 01:53:32.663
thinking about patients,

01:53:32.663 --> 01:53:34.440
you are spending time
thinking about, well,

01:53:34.440 --> 01:53:36.103
how do I get this new thing done?

01:53:37.100 --> 01:53:39.150
These are new relationships I have.

01:53:39.150 --> 01:53:41.670
And it's a big change process.

01:53:41.670 --> 01:53:45.890
And we shouldn't underestimate the support

01:53:45.890 --> 01:53:47.490
that will be required.

01:53:47.490 --> 01:53:52.310
The second is around data
and the critical importance

01:53:52.310 --> 01:53:57.310
of using information that you
collect on a regular basis

01:53:57.870 --> 01:53:59.970
to constantly check in.

01:53:59.970 --> 01:54:03.800
And I heard this very clearly
from Masood, at Hall Green.

01:54:03.800 --> 01:54:08.010
They had weekly meetings in
which they actually tested

01:54:08.010 --> 01:54:12.200
how well they were performing
against their goals

01:54:12.200 --> 01:54:15.820
for meeting patient access
and experience measures.

01:54:15.820 --> 01:54:19.650
And I think that has got to be,

01:54:19.650 --> 01:54:23.660
a central feature of the
support of practises.

01:54:23.660 --> 01:54:27.220
Is making sure that they have
the information that they need

01:54:27.220 --> 01:54:30.550
presented to them in a
clear and understandable way

01:54:30.550 --> 01:54:33.960
that allows them to make
sure that they're on track

01:54:33.960 --> 01:54:35.740
and moving forward.

01:54:35.740 --> 01:54:38.850
So Claire, kudos to you on all the work,

01:54:38.850 --> 01:54:41.533
I really look forward to
seeing the final product.

01:54:42.770 --> 01:54:43.603
- Tim, thank you.

01:54:43.603 --> 01:54:46.653
Let me just come in, if I might,

01:54:48.090 --> 01:54:50.700
by way of closure on
this, or although clearly

01:54:50.700 --> 01:54:53.910
we look forward to seeing
the report once published

01:54:53.910 --> 01:54:55.043
and getting behind it.

01:54:56.510 --> 01:54:57.807
What to say really?

01:54:59.022 --> 01:55:02.370
I was struck when I first
came in and someone's

01:55:02.370 --> 01:55:05.580
just used a statistic that
over 90% of the population's

01:55:05.580 --> 01:55:08.180
engagement with the health
services in primary care.

01:55:09.400 --> 01:55:12.450
And I would also say, which
is not a criticism, at all,

01:55:12.450 --> 01:55:14.230
was Ian has done a superb job.

01:55:14.230 --> 01:55:17.280
I think leading as one
of his responsibilities,

01:55:17.280 --> 01:55:19.690
the work into primary care.

01:55:19.690 --> 01:55:22.920
But one things I personally
want to do is to see the voice

01:55:22.920 --> 01:55:25.770
of primary care louder in NHS England.

01:55:26.930 --> 01:55:28.870
Partly because of that 90%.

01:55:28.870 --> 01:55:30.790
But also partly I think
because of the things

01:55:30.790 --> 01:55:34.510
that Claire has been talking about.

01:55:34.510 --> 01:55:39.510
About a sort of a
multidisciplinary service delivery

01:55:39.830 --> 01:55:43.223
in the community through
the primary care practises.

01:55:44.807 --> 01:55:48.530
Probably, in my rather
layman's and simplistic view

01:55:48.530 --> 01:55:51.770
revolving around the GPs as the senior,

01:55:51.770 --> 01:55:53.950
most experienced relevant clinicians,

01:55:53.950 --> 01:55:58.730
but orchestrating a wider set of services

01:55:59.990 --> 01:56:02.530
or signposting to a wider set of services

01:56:02.530 --> 01:56:06.423
available to their local
population in their community.

01:56:07.410 --> 01:56:12.410
And in many ways, and sounding
a bit, not glib, actually.

01:56:13.455 --> 01:56:16.700
I can hardly think of a better

01:56:16.700 --> 01:56:18.950
or more aspirational societal role.

01:56:18.950 --> 01:56:20.650
And I apologise to all the hospital,

01:56:20.650 --> 01:56:22.410
clinicians and specialists, as I say it.

01:56:22.410 --> 01:56:27.410
But I can hardly think of a
more important societal role

01:56:28.080 --> 01:56:32.700
aspirational role, than
actually being that primary care

01:56:32.700 --> 01:56:36.290
provider and coordinator with
the delivery of primary care

01:56:36.290 --> 01:56:41.290
when 90% of the population's
engagement is in primary.

01:56:42.780 --> 01:56:45.547
And I'm not being pollyannish
because it's quite clear.

01:56:45.547 --> 01:56:48.580
And one of certainly my
part is primary care access,

01:56:48.580 --> 01:56:51.537
which is a significant
challenge at the moment.

01:56:51.537 --> 01:56:54.030
And we are going to work
hugely hard with quick action,

01:56:54.030 --> 01:56:57.480
quick wins, I hope to see
how we can alleviate that.

01:56:57.480 --> 01:56:58.833
But at the same time,

01:56:59.930 --> 01:57:02.640
there was something like over
between 31 and 32 million

01:57:02.640 --> 01:57:05.860
appointments, GP appointments in March.

01:57:05.860 --> 01:57:10.860
62% of those were delivered face to face.

01:57:11.034 --> 01:57:12.930
And then when I think
about the structure Claire,

01:57:12.930 --> 01:57:13.800
that you're looking at.

01:57:13.800 --> 01:57:15.400
And I absolutely agree with you.

01:57:17.256 --> 01:57:18.764
I think that the thought
of the collaboration

01:57:18.764 --> 01:57:22.370
within the integrated care
systems and the importance given

01:57:22.370 --> 01:57:25.220
by the chairs and CEOs of
those systems and those Boards

01:57:25.220 --> 01:57:28.910
to primary care within
a collaborating system,

01:57:28.910 --> 01:57:31.001
I think is really powerful.

01:57:31.001 --> 01:57:33.260
I keep getting all my stats.

01:57:33.260 --> 01:57:34.430
I've tried to get the shape of it.

01:57:34.430 --> 01:57:36.813
So 38,000 GPs,

01:57:37.930 --> 01:57:39.885
6,700 practises,

01:57:39.885 --> 01:57:43.770
between 1,200 and 1,250 PCNs.

01:57:43.770 --> 01:57:46.960
And an ability to cooperate
across those PCNs in delivering

01:57:46.960 --> 01:57:49.160
service to the community.

01:57:49.160 --> 01:57:52.253
I think there's a real prize here.

01:57:54.545 --> 01:57:57.810
And it's a real prize in
support of our workforce,

01:57:57.810 --> 01:57:59.750
which is probably feeling
under a lot of pressure,

01:57:59.750 --> 01:58:02.170
but which is right at
the forefront, I think,

01:58:02.170 --> 01:58:06.630
of what we can aspire to
deliver in terms of population

01:58:06.630 --> 01:58:08.670
health and community health.

01:58:08.670 --> 01:58:12.110
So I think it's a really
important, truly important

01:58:13.220 --> 01:58:16.710
exercise that we are now embarking upon.

01:58:16.710 --> 01:58:20.820
So I want to speak very loudly
for the GPs and GP practises.

01:58:20.820 --> 01:58:22.433
And I know that Amanda, you do.

01:58:23.885 --> 01:58:27.870
Claire, I'm looking forward to the report.

01:58:27.870 --> 01:58:31.333
We're going to get behind
you and behind it on it.

01:58:32.960 --> 01:58:35.060
I'm interested to understand continuity

01:58:35.060 --> 01:58:38.020
and how that gets defined
and offered and operated.

01:58:38.020 --> 01:58:39.850
And there are different
views on the spectrum

01:58:39.850 --> 01:58:41.860
of what is continuity.

01:58:41.860 --> 01:58:43.770
Is it the GP with every single patient,

01:58:43.770 --> 01:58:46.530
or is it complex long
term developed conditions

01:58:46.530 --> 01:58:50.040
where the GP is directly
the owner in inverted commas

01:58:50.040 --> 01:58:50.873
of the patient?

01:58:50.873 --> 01:58:53.264
So there are different
models of delivering that.

01:58:53.264 --> 01:58:56.810
But I think this is a really
important topic to get at.

01:58:56.810 --> 01:58:58.573
And it's not just the access point,

01:58:59.880 --> 01:59:01.740
which is the first part of it.

01:59:01.740 --> 01:59:05.040
It's that evolving model
into the medium term

01:59:05.040 --> 01:59:06.680
of how this gets better offered.

01:59:06.680 --> 01:59:10.600
But I do think it's a remarkable
role, the role of a GP,

01:59:10.600 --> 01:59:15.600
and the role of a, sorry, a
primary care service provider.

01:59:16.850 --> 01:59:17.800
Not necessarily a GP,

01:59:17.800 --> 01:59:20.920
whether it's a paramedic
or a practise nurse,

01:59:20.920 --> 01:59:23.290
a physiotherapist, whatever it is.

01:59:23.290 --> 01:59:25.730
This is a really important
exercise we're embarking upon.

01:59:25.730 --> 01:59:30.025
So Claire for your work,
thank you very much, actually.

01:59:30.025 --> 01:59:32.525
And as say, we look forward
to getting the report.

01:59:33.400 --> 01:59:35.800
So can I quickly move us on.

01:59:35.800 --> 01:59:40.650
We have one important item
to, just to close with,

01:59:40.650 --> 01:59:42.320
which is the national guardians office.

01:59:42.320 --> 01:59:44.976
So we've got Jayne Chidgey-Clark coming,

01:59:44.976 --> 01:59:48.670
and the NGO it's jointly
funded by CQC and NHSEI.

01:59:51.570 --> 01:59:53.550
And they have an annual
report to the Board.

01:59:53.550 --> 01:59:56.530
And this is Jayne's chance,
I think, to reflect with us

01:59:56.530 --> 01:59:59.770
on progress made since March of 21,

01:59:59.770 --> 02:00:03.360
and also the key priorities of
the national guardians office

02:00:03.360 --> 02:00:05.220
looking forward into the forthcoming year.

02:00:05.220 --> 02:00:08.860
So Jayne, if I can hand to you for a very

02:00:08.860 --> 02:00:11.351
relatively succinct intro to that topic,

02:00:11.351 --> 02:00:12.184
that would be really good.

02:00:12.184 --> 02:00:13.150
Thank you.

02:00:13.150 --> 02:00:14.560
- Thank you very much chair.

02:00:14.560 --> 02:00:17.580
And thank you Board members
for welcoming here today.

02:00:17.580 --> 02:00:18.470
It's a real privilege.

02:00:18.470 --> 02:00:20.840
And obviously the first time
I've come as the new national

02:00:20.840 --> 02:00:24.010
guardian following in the
footsteps of Dr. Henrietta Hughes,

02:00:24.010 --> 02:00:25.610
who led the national Guardian's office

02:00:25.610 --> 02:00:28.230
for the previous six
years and was instrumental

02:00:28.230 --> 02:00:33.185
in setting up the network
which from zero to now over 820

02:00:33.185 --> 02:00:36.560
trained guardians across
a number of organisations,

02:00:36.560 --> 02:00:40.010
both within the NHS independent
healthcare hospices,

02:00:40.010 --> 02:00:44.760
primary medical services and moving out.

02:00:44.760 --> 02:00:46.070
So it's an absolute privilege to be here.

02:00:46.070 --> 02:00:47.843
And I don't intend to
repeat what's in the report.

02:00:47.843 --> 02:00:49.930
I just want to make a few reflections

02:00:49.930 --> 02:00:51.620
for you from my perspective,

02:00:51.620 --> 02:00:54.290
as the new guardian and
having been in post now

02:00:54.290 --> 02:00:55.980
around six months.

02:00:55.980 --> 02:01:00.340
And a bit of a state of
the nation and to reflect

02:01:00.340 --> 02:01:04.250
in order to encourage the Board of NHSEI,

02:01:04.250 --> 02:01:06.637
in absolutely setting that
tone around speaker culture

02:01:06.637 --> 02:01:09.000
and the importance of it,

02:01:09.000 --> 02:01:12.210
the report is clear in
some of the outcomes

02:01:12.210 --> 02:01:13.210
from this last year.

02:01:14.102 --> 02:01:16.129
And obviously we've worked
along as every other paper

02:01:16.129 --> 02:01:18.310
you've had today in the
backdrop of the pandemic.

02:01:18.310 --> 02:01:21.680
We didn't do any particular
additional reports

02:01:21.680 --> 02:01:22.513
this last year.

02:01:22.513 --> 02:01:24.300
In the first year of pandemic,

02:01:24.300 --> 02:01:26.010
we did pulse surveys with our guardians

02:01:26.010 --> 02:01:28.230
to find out what impact pandemic

02:01:28.230 --> 02:01:29.800
might be having on speaking up.

02:01:29.800 --> 02:01:32.540
This year, it's more
been business as usual

02:01:32.540 --> 02:01:34.100
in the sense of we didn't do

02:01:34.100 --> 02:01:36.320
any particular additional reports.

02:01:36.320 --> 02:01:39.300
However, we continue to see the key themes

02:01:39.300 --> 02:01:40.730
that came out in the previous year.

02:01:40.730 --> 02:01:43.350
Still with bullying and harassment,

02:01:43.350 --> 02:01:44.920
being one of the key areas where guardians

02:01:44.920 --> 02:01:47.217
are taking matters that
people are raising,

02:01:47.217 --> 02:01:49.716
worker safety, patient safety.

02:01:49.716 --> 02:01:52.020
And interestingly for me,

02:01:52.020 --> 02:01:54.590
seeing some of the softer
data coming through

02:01:55.511 --> 02:01:57.263
on the qualitative side,
on some of those areas,

02:01:58.338 --> 02:01:59.450
a lot that doesn't reach
the bullying and harassment

02:01:59.450 --> 02:02:01.350
threshold, but the civility.

02:02:01.350 --> 02:02:03.420
The way people are being spoken to,

02:02:03.420 --> 02:02:05.680
either within teams or from leadership

02:02:05.680 --> 02:02:07.260
and managers to workers.

02:02:07.260 --> 02:02:10.300
And that feels really
important that we know, I mean,

02:02:10.300 --> 02:02:12.664
you have a large director
for improving culture

02:02:12.664 --> 02:02:15.534
within the NHS that I'm
privileged to work alongside.

02:02:15.534 --> 02:02:17.787
And we absolutely share those values

02:02:17.787 --> 02:02:20.440
that we need to improve the situation,

02:02:20.440 --> 02:02:23.290
improve by our own role modelling

02:02:23.290 --> 02:02:25.060
and ensure that leaders are doing

02:02:25.060 --> 02:02:26.623
the right thing by workers.

02:02:27.810 --> 02:02:31.500
So from this last year,
there's been a growth

02:02:31.500 --> 02:02:33.550
in more guardians being trained

02:02:33.550 --> 02:02:37.020
as the guardian model is rolled out.

02:02:37.020 --> 02:02:38.510
And that's a really good thing.

02:02:38.510 --> 02:02:41.255
However, I did say in the report,

02:02:41.255 --> 02:02:42.864
one of the things that is concerning still

02:02:42.864 --> 02:02:45.122
is the inconsistency within which the role

02:02:45.122 --> 02:02:46.950
is being implemented.

02:02:46.950 --> 02:02:49.075
And there are still some organisations

02:02:49.075 --> 02:02:51.660
that are not implementing

02:02:51.660 --> 02:02:54.530
it in line with the recommendations.

02:02:54.530 --> 02:02:56.490
Some people don't have
dedicated time, for example.

02:02:56.490 --> 02:02:58.360
Don't have dedicated resources.

02:02:58.360 --> 02:03:01.480
Some guardians are telling
us that their chief execs

02:03:01.480 --> 02:03:03.820
or their non exec leads for speak up.

02:03:03.820 --> 02:03:06.100
Don't really know what their role is.

02:03:06.100 --> 02:03:07.810
Thankfully, that's a minority,

02:03:07.810 --> 02:03:11.640
but even to hear that it's a
minority is a worry for me.

02:03:11.640 --> 02:03:14.160
The other concern that I
absolutely wanted to speak

02:03:14.160 --> 02:03:17.470
to you today about is the
potential early warning signs.

02:03:17.470 --> 02:03:18.870
Now I'm not a shroud waiver,

02:03:19.838 --> 02:03:21.410
but there is a decrease in
the confidence in workers,

02:03:21.410 --> 02:03:23.520
both through the NHS staff survey,

02:03:23.520 --> 02:03:26.220
the four questions that
speak to confidence

02:03:26.220 --> 02:03:30.150
in raising matters, getting in
the way of doing a good job,

02:03:30.150 --> 02:03:31.670
as well as our own guardian survey.

02:03:31.670 --> 02:03:34.520
And both of those this
year for the first time

02:03:34.520 --> 02:03:37.500
are showing a decrease
in competence of staff.

02:03:37.500 --> 02:03:41.060
And whilst that's a sort of
four, five percent decrease

02:03:41.060 --> 02:03:44.100
that is still in a large sample, a worry.

02:03:44.100 --> 02:03:47.280
And obviously we don't want
to see that continuing.

02:03:47.280 --> 02:03:49.240
Why do we think that's happening?

02:03:49.240 --> 02:03:51.360
I think we think that is because people

02:03:51.360 --> 02:03:53.440
are still afraid of the futility

02:03:53.440 --> 02:03:55.030
that I speak up and nothing changes.

02:03:55.030 --> 02:03:57.130
So it's evidencing that people are heard

02:03:57.130 --> 02:04:00.110
and matters are listened to
and responded to appropriately.

02:04:00.110 --> 02:04:04.320
And the second worrying trend,
is the high profile cases,

02:04:04.320 --> 02:04:07.650
where workers may speak up and
then they suffer detriment.

02:04:07.650 --> 02:04:09.710
And that fear of detriment
is still very much there.

02:04:09.710 --> 02:04:11.925
Our guardians are telling us that.

02:04:11.925 --> 02:04:16.000
So that wider piece of the
potentially worrying signs

02:04:16.000 --> 02:04:19.290
from these two important
surveys mean that there's still

02:04:19.290 --> 02:04:20.200
so much more to do.

02:04:20.200 --> 02:04:23.210
And I look to the Board
of NHS England alongside

02:04:23.210 --> 02:04:27.450
our voice to be talking to
leaders about the importance

02:04:27.450 --> 02:04:29.290
of speaking up and
getting the culture right

02:04:29.290 --> 02:04:31.038
within their organisations.

02:04:31.038 --> 02:04:32.787
And that includes national
bodies as well as,

02:04:32.787 --> 02:04:36.293
and the regulators as well as
individual service providers.

02:04:37.320 --> 02:04:40.370
I set out in the report, the
priorities for this year,

02:04:40.370 --> 02:04:45.370
and they continued to be
aligned to the four pillars

02:04:47.060 --> 02:04:49.451
of the strategic framework for the NGO.

02:04:49.451 --> 02:04:52.410
That was published last
year, well, before I came.

02:04:52.410 --> 02:04:54.200
So that's looking at worker,

02:04:54.200 --> 02:04:57.070
are workers able do they know
how to, and able to speak up.

02:04:57.070 --> 02:04:57.980
Looking at guardians.

02:04:57.980 --> 02:05:01.820
We lead the guardian network
and are they well supported

02:05:01.820 --> 02:05:03.010
is the role being implemented.

02:05:03.010 --> 02:05:05.840
And we work very closely with
your freedom speak up team

02:05:05.840 --> 02:05:07.520
within NHS England with respect

02:05:07.520 --> 02:05:09.690
to that new policy that is coming.

02:05:09.690 --> 02:05:12.290
And I want to make a plea
today that is a Board.

02:05:12.290 --> 02:05:14.020
I know you have very robust arrangements

02:05:14.020 --> 02:05:16.510
for your own internal
speak up arrangements.

02:05:16.510 --> 02:05:19.070
But I want to encourage you
to use that new guidance

02:05:19.070 --> 02:05:22.180
both to be promoting it
out in NHS organisations

02:05:22.180 --> 02:05:24.190
and those that have an NHS contract,

02:05:24.190 --> 02:05:27.130
as well as within your
own organisation too,

02:05:27.130 --> 02:05:28.980
to do a stock check against that guidance

02:05:28.980 --> 02:05:31.640
and that the implementation
tool for Boards

02:05:31.640 --> 02:05:33.030
that will go alongside it.

02:05:33.030 --> 02:05:36.086
Because I really firmly
believe that as leaders,

02:05:36.086 --> 02:05:37.070
we set the tone,

02:05:37.070 --> 02:05:40.600
we set the culture and we
need to be across all areas

02:05:40.600 --> 02:05:42.533
of the organisation, both your own,

02:05:43.530 --> 02:05:46.300
and our own and individual
organisations checking that.

02:05:46.300 --> 02:05:49.080
I had the privilege of speaking
to a Board at NHS trust

02:05:49.080 --> 02:05:50.380
the other the other week.

02:05:50.380 --> 02:05:54.640
And I was saying to them,
your rate is good by CQC.

02:05:54.640 --> 02:05:57.250
You have good feedback
on your staff survey.

02:05:57.250 --> 02:05:59.625
However, are you confident
that across all areas.

02:05:59.625 --> 02:06:03.610
if I went and spoke to a team
over there that work at night

02:06:03.610 --> 02:06:05.417
or a team over there,

02:06:05.417 --> 02:06:07.470
out in the community that
your speak up culture

02:06:07.470 --> 02:06:09.720
is consistent across
the whole organisation.

02:06:09.720 --> 02:06:12.810
So even for those that are
good, we want to see and ensure

02:06:12.810 --> 02:06:15.460
that that's embedded really fully,

02:06:15.460 --> 02:06:17.700
not just across the mainstream.

02:06:17.700 --> 02:06:20.440
And obviously for those that
are having more challenges

02:06:20.440 --> 02:06:21.490
around their culture.

02:06:22.436 --> 02:06:24.270
And we see that in some of
the highly publicised reports

02:06:24.270 --> 02:06:26.790
that that's improving and people are open

02:06:26.790 --> 02:06:27.630
to looking and saying,

02:06:27.630 --> 02:06:29.710
what's getting in the way
of people feeling confident

02:06:29.710 --> 02:06:30.543
to speak up.

02:06:30.543 --> 02:06:31.860
And most importantly,

02:06:31.860 --> 02:06:33.710
that it's acted upon and listened on.

02:06:34.830 --> 02:06:36.120
Great to see Navina Evans here,

02:06:36.120 --> 02:06:37.610
from health education in England today,

02:06:37.610 --> 02:06:40.180
because we've just recently
published our third piece

02:06:40.180 --> 02:06:42.030
of training and that's for leaders.

02:06:42.030 --> 02:06:43.570
And I would like to encourage you,

02:06:43.570 --> 02:06:45.880
if you haven't already as a
Board to have an opportunity

02:06:45.880 --> 02:06:47.960
to discuss that short leadership training

02:06:47.960 --> 02:06:50.140
and the implications
of that for yourselves,

02:06:50.140 --> 02:06:52.560
and to be able to promote
that amongst the organisations

02:06:52.560 --> 02:06:53.743
that you lead.

02:06:55.127 --> 02:06:56.460
- Jayne, thanks, oh, I'm sorry.

02:06:56.460 --> 02:06:58.280
- By way, I was just going
to say, by way of conclusion,

02:06:58.280 --> 02:07:01.000
I'm really happy to take
any comments on the paper.

02:07:01.000 --> 02:07:01.913
And just to say,

02:07:03.273 --> 02:07:06.110
there's a lot of good
work going on to improve

02:07:06.110 --> 02:07:07.840
Speak up culture, but
there's some worrying signs

02:07:07.840 --> 02:07:09.170
that we can't rest on our laurels,

02:07:09.170 --> 02:07:10.680
and we all need to be working as leaders

02:07:10.680 --> 02:07:11.780
to do more about this.

02:07:13.050 --> 02:07:14.780
- And pause and see if
there any comments, I mean,

02:07:14.780 --> 02:07:18.010
mind would be that I
think you are quite right

02:07:18.010 --> 02:07:21.800
that a good Speak up culture
is an essentially ingredient

02:07:21.800 --> 02:07:23.067
of any good culture.

02:07:23.067 --> 02:07:26.059
And certainly those of us coming,
well, I come from outside,

02:07:26.059 --> 02:07:28.717
financial services background
and the speak up culture

02:07:28.717 --> 02:07:30.520
and the development of
that and the promotion

02:07:30.520 --> 02:07:34.350
of that for organisations and
leaders of those organisations

02:07:34.350 --> 02:07:36.440
is key critical, actually.

02:07:36.440 --> 02:07:39.470
So I think your report
resonates very strongly.

02:07:39.470 --> 02:07:41.150
And I spent not just with me,

02:07:41.150 --> 02:07:43.510
but with Amanda and her top team

02:07:43.510 --> 02:07:46.600
and with my other colleagues.

02:07:46.600 --> 02:07:51.600
And we note your report and
impact also the worrying signs.

02:07:52.520 --> 02:07:53.860
So we need to be watching that.

02:07:53.860 --> 02:07:56.150
Em, you want to make a
comment, it's going to a brief

02:07:56.150 --> 02:07:57.750
comment 'cause we're
going to run out of time.

02:07:57.750 --> 02:08:01.332
But Em, you want to ask a
question or make a comment.

02:08:01.332 --> 02:08:03.120
- It's just a comment actually.

02:08:03.120 --> 02:08:04.750
So working together with Jayne,

02:08:04.750 --> 02:08:07.276
we have got this guide that's developed

02:08:07.276 --> 02:08:09.100
and aimed it, leaders.

02:08:09.100 --> 02:08:10.860
And actually it's great
that we are probably going

02:08:10.860 --> 02:08:13.140
to be publishing that at
the end of this month.

02:08:13.140 --> 02:08:18.140
And that is a real practical
guide about how can leaders

02:08:19.110 --> 02:08:21.870
both reflect on their own
organisational cultures,

02:08:21.870 --> 02:08:23.270
their own leadership behaviours.

02:08:23.270 --> 02:08:27.000
And then how can we really
make sure that we've reached

02:08:27.000 --> 02:08:29.730
the voices of the most seldom heard?

02:08:29.730 --> 02:08:32.120
So it's good news, Jayne,

02:08:32.120 --> 02:08:34.080
that we've got to the
point where hopefully,

02:08:34.080 --> 02:08:36.330
that's going to be published
at the end of this month.

02:08:36.330 --> 02:08:39.193
So I wanted to just put
that in front of the Board.

02:08:40.420 --> 02:08:41.253
- Thank you.

02:08:41.253 --> 02:08:42.790
And Jayne, thank you
very much for the report

02:08:42.790 --> 02:08:44.370
and coming to talk to us about it.

02:08:44.370 --> 02:08:45.380
I appreciate that.

02:08:45.380 --> 02:08:46.550
Thank you very much.

02:08:46.550 --> 02:08:49.740
So, I'm going to pause to see
if there's any other business

02:08:49.740 --> 02:08:52.760
for many of the non-exec
Board members first,

02:08:52.760 --> 02:08:55.580
and then if not,

02:08:55.580 --> 02:08:58.777
Amanda, is there anything you want by AOB,

02:08:58.777 --> 02:09:00.580
potentially, we look forward?

02:09:00.580 --> 02:09:02.350
Are there any thoughts
that you have that we might

02:09:02.350 --> 02:09:05.330
be thinking to look at?

02:09:05.330 --> 02:09:06.450
- Yes, thank you, Richard.

02:09:06.450 --> 02:09:07.770
So, I mean,

02:09:07.770 --> 02:09:09.652
I think we've covered a
huge amount of ground today,

02:09:09.652 --> 02:09:10.950
and there's an awful lot
to come back to of course,

02:09:10.950 --> 02:09:11.840
in future meetings.

02:09:11.840 --> 02:09:15.150
But the one that I think we
might want to log for our next

02:09:15.150 --> 02:09:18.265
conversation in July, is
particularly to come back

02:09:18.265 --> 02:09:22.500
on given kind of your
comments about capacities

02:09:22.500 --> 02:09:24.251
some of my opening remarks about pressure,

02:09:24.251 --> 02:09:27.180
particularly, around
urgent emergency care.

02:09:27.180 --> 02:09:29.510
Some of the comments we've
just referred to in David's

02:09:29.510 --> 02:09:32.750
report about the upcoming deep
dive on urgent emergency care

02:09:32.750 --> 02:09:34.810
in particular and the
way that that connects

02:09:34.810 --> 02:09:37.290
through so many of the other
themes we've discussed today.

02:09:37.290 --> 02:09:39.940
I do wonder if perhaps next
time we might bring back

02:09:39.940 --> 02:09:42.920
a discussion about
planning ahead for winter.

02:09:42.920 --> 02:09:47.920
Because, even though it's
only what May, at the moment,

02:09:48.070 --> 02:09:52.280
it will not be long before
winter is upon us again.

02:09:52.280 --> 02:09:54.750
- Amanda, I think that would
be, and you and I have,

02:09:54.750 --> 02:09:58.150
you currently raised that with
me in earlier discussions,

02:09:58.150 --> 02:09:59.150
and I think that would be,

02:09:59.150 --> 02:10:02.490
even though it will be a
midsummer July discussion.

02:10:02.490 --> 02:10:04.877
I think it's very timely
to be looking forward here

02:10:04.877 --> 02:10:07.400
and thinking very hard about winter,

02:10:07.400 --> 02:10:10.060
the provision of health
service through the winter

02:10:10.060 --> 02:10:12.520
and what that will take.

02:10:12.520 --> 02:10:13.560
So I think that's a very good idea.

02:10:13.560 --> 02:10:14.940
Thank you, Amanda.

02:10:14.940 --> 02:10:17.530
So let me thank Amanda and the executives,

02:10:17.530 --> 02:10:20.870
very much for all the hard
work that you are doing.

02:10:20.870 --> 02:10:23.250
My thanks to the Board.

02:10:23.250 --> 02:10:26.020
Because I have this privilege of chair.

02:10:26.020 --> 02:10:31.010
I just want to finish just
by just repeating actually,

02:10:31.010 --> 02:10:34.790
where I think I am after
this first six weeks or so,

02:10:34.790 --> 02:10:37.770
which is those three first priorities.

02:10:37.770 --> 02:10:40.480
And by saying them, it doesn't
mean that other aspects

02:10:40.480 --> 02:10:42.080
I've already mention,
not really important,

02:10:42.080 --> 02:10:45.490
or that we're going to slow
down our focus on them.

02:10:45.490 --> 02:10:47.240
However, on a personal note that,

02:10:47.240 --> 02:10:50.220
looking at the elective backlog hard,

02:10:50.220 --> 02:10:52.860
looking at urgent and emergency care hard

02:10:52.860 --> 02:10:56.702
and primary care access are important.

02:10:56.702 --> 02:10:59.530
There are a couple of
what I call machinery,

02:10:59.530 --> 02:11:01.590
but it's really big and complex machinery,

02:11:01.590 --> 02:11:04.060
which is the merge of the five entities,

02:11:04.060 --> 02:11:07.050
which we have to give
effect to by end of March

02:11:07.050 --> 02:11:08.250
of next year.

02:11:08.250 --> 02:11:11.980
And it's the implementation of
the integrated care systems,

02:11:11.980 --> 02:11:13.480
the Boards and the partnerships.

02:11:13.480 --> 02:11:17.270
Again, we need they're coming
to life in very few days,

02:11:17.270 --> 02:11:18.930
time, very few weeks.

02:11:18.930 --> 02:11:23.930
And then those priorities
are underpinned in my mind

02:11:23.930 --> 02:11:26.920
by the very necessary
work around workforce.

02:11:26.920 --> 02:11:29.200
Long term planning around workforce

02:11:29.200 --> 02:11:31.630
and short term
considerations of workforce.

02:11:31.630 --> 02:11:34.420
But it's long term
planning around workforce

02:11:34.420 --> 02:11:36.800
and then this single word capacity.

02:11:36.800 --> 02:11:39.200
And so what is the capacity of the service

02:11:39.200 --> 02:11:43.110
to meet the demand and likely
growing demand upon it?

02:11:43.110 --> 02:11:45.227
And how should we think about that,

02:11:45.227 --> 02:11:49.060
and how can we lift our
capacity or change the shape

02:11:49.060 --> 02:11:51.277
of our capacity to meet
that future demand.

02:11:51.277 --> 02:11:53.450
And that may be more
beds, maybe more scanners,

02:11:53.450 --> 02:11:55.550
whatever it is, or might
be working practise change,

02:11:55.550 --> 02:11:57.310
or it's how we deliver technology.

02:11:57.310 --> 02:11:59.960
That's a really rich and important theme.

02:11:59.960 --> 02:12:01.650
So if I can finish with that,

02:12:01.650 --> 02:12:04.370
and just my thanks to
everybody for joining.

02:12:04.370 --> 02:12:07.200
I'll call the meeting to a close

02:12:07.200 --> 02:12:08.840
and I look forward to seeing you all,

02:12:08.840 --> 02:12:10.440
well, many of you actually next Wednesday

02:12:10.440 --> 02:12:13.070
for their deep dive on
urgent emergency care.

02:12:13.070 --> 02:12:14.220
So thank you very much.

